import{n as e,r as t,t as n}from"./helpers-ClX0bpsp.js";var r={building:`archive-annex`,overview:[n(`Protecting data is not only about stopping attackers. It is also about getting data back after an accident, a deletion or a failure, and keeping it for as long as your retention rules require. This building covers the AWS tools for that: {{term:aws-backup|AWS Backup}} for centralised backups, S3 versioning and replication for object data, and Amazon Macie for finding out what kind of data you are keeping.`,[`backup-what-is`,`s3-versioning`,`s3-replication`,`macie`],[`backup-what-is|AWS Backup is a fully-managed service that makes it easy to centralize and automate data protection across AWS services, in the cloud, and on premises.`,`s3-versioning|You can use the S3 Versioning feature to preserve, retrieve, and restore every version of every object stored in your buckets.`,`s3-replication|The destination buckets can be in different AWS Regions or within the same Region as the source bucket.`,`macie|Amazon Macie is a data security service that discovers sensitive data by using machine learning and pattern matching`])],bullets:[{id:`1.3-K2`,concepts:[n(`Recovery needs a copy that survives the failure. S3 Versioning preserves, retrieves and restores every version of every object, so you can recover from unintended user actions and application failures, including accidental deletion or overwrite.`,`s3-versioning`,[`You can use the S3 Versioning feature to preserve, retrieve, and restore every version of every object stored in your buckets.`,`With versioning you can recover more easily from both unintended user actions and application failures.`]),n(`AWS Backup lets you automate and consolidate backup tasks that were previously done service by service, removing the need for custom scripts and manual processes. AWS notes that it does not govern backups you take outside AWS Backup.`,`backup-what-is`,[`It allows you to automate and consolidate backup tasks that were previously performed service-by-service, and removes the need to create custom scripts and manual processes.`,`AWS Backup does not govern backups you take in your AWS environment outside of AWS Backup.`])],services:[`AWS Backup`,`Amazon S3`],design:[n(`Use versioning on important buckets to guard against deletes and overwrites, and AWS Backup for services such as DynamoDB tables and EFS file systems that are covered by a backup plan.`,[`s3-versioning`,`backup-plans`],[`s3-versioning|Versioning-enabled buckets can help you recover objects from accidental deletion or overwrite.`,`backup-plans|In AWS Backup, a backup plan is a policy expression that defines when and how you want to back up your AWS resources, such as Amazon DynamoDB tables or Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS) file systems.`])]},{id:`1.3-K3`,concepts:[n(`Retention is how long data is kept. A backup plan sets the schedule and retention, and AWS Backup manages the lifecycle of the backups according to your retention settings. Resources assigned to the plan are backed up and retained automatically.`,`backup-plans`,[`You can assign resources to backup plans, and AWS Backup automatically backs up and retains backups for those resources according to the backup plan.`,`AWS Backup also seamlessly manages your backup plan's lifecycle based on your retention settings`]),n(`Classification is knowing what the data is. Amazon Macie automates discovery, logging and reporting of sensitive data in your S3 data estate, either by automated sensitive data discovery or by sensitive data discovery jobs.`,`macie-discovery`,[`With Amazon Macie, you can automate discovery, logging, and reporting of sensitive data in your Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) data estate.`,`by configuring Macie to perform automated sensitive data discovery, and by creating and running sensitive data discovery jobs.`]),n(`To hold data immutably for a required period, S3 Object Lock uses a write-once-read-many model and works only in buckets that have S3 Versioning enabled. Backups in a locked AWS Backup vault cannot be deleted until their lifecycle completes.`,[`s3-object-lock`,`backup-vault-lock`],[`s3-object-lock|Object Lock uses a write-once-read-many (WORM) model to store objects.`,`s3-object-lock|Object Lock works only in buckets that have S3 Versioning enabled.`,`backup-vault-lock|Because backups in a locked vault cannot be deleted until their lifecycle completes, ensure your retention settings are configured appropriately to avoid unintended storage costs.`]),n(`Both locks have two modes, and the modes differ in who can undo them. In S3 Object Lock compliance mode, a protected object version cannot be overwritten or deleted by any user, including the root user; in governance mode, users with special permissions can alter lock settings or delete the object. AWS Backup Vault Lock has the same two names: a vault locked in governance mode can be managed or deleted by users with the appropriate IAM permissions, whereas a vault in compliance mode becomes immutable once its lock takes effect.`,[`s3-object-lock`,`backup-vault-lock`],[`s3-object-lock|In compliance mode, a protected object version can't be overwritten or deleted by any user, including the root user in your AWS account.`,`s3-object-lock|In governance mode, users can't overwrite or delete an object version or alter its lock settings unless they have special permissions.`,`backup-vault-lock|When you create a vault lock, you have a choice of two modes: Governance mode or Compliance mode.`,`backup-vault-lock|A vault locked in Governance mode can be managed or deleted by users who have the appropriate IAM permissions.`,`backup-vault-lock|Once a vault in compliance mode is locked, it is immutable, meaning the lock cannot be removed`],{allow:[`two`]})],services:[`AWS Backup`,`Amazon Macie`,`Amazon S3`],design:[n(`One way to work: use Macie to find sensitive data in S3, then decide what protection the buckets that hold it need. Macie generates a finding when it detects sensitive data in an S3 object, and that finding is what tells you where to look.`,`macie`,[`If Macie detects sensitive data in an S3 object, Macie generates a finding to notify you of the sensitive data that it found.`])]},{id:`1.3-S5`,concepts:[n(`S3 replication copies objects to destination buckets in the same or a different AWS Region, and to buckets owned by the same or a different account. Live replication copies new and updated objects as they are written, and both the source and destination buckets must have versioning enabled.`,[`s3-replication`,`s3-replication-requirements`],[`s3-replication-requirements|Both source and destination buckets must have versioning enabled.`,`s3-replication|The destination buckets can be in different AWS Regions or within the same Region as the source bucket.`,`s3-replication|Live replication – To automatically replicate new and updated objects as they are written to the source bucket, use live replication.`,`s3-replication|Buckets that are configured for object replication can be owned by the same AWS account or by different accounts.`]),n(`AWS Backup can copy backups across Regions on demand or as part of a plan, which AWS says is valuable for business continuity or compliance requirements to store backups a minimum distance away from production data. It can also manage backups across all accounts in an AWS Organizations structure.`,`backup-what-is`,[`Using AWS Backup, you can copy backups to multiple different AWS Regions on demand or automatically as part of a scheduled backup plan.`,`Cross-Region backup is particularly valuable if you have business continuity or compliance requirements to store backups a minimum distance away from your production data.`,`You can use AWS Backup to manage your backups across all AWS accounts inside your AWS Organizations structure.`])],services:[`AWS Backup`,`Amazon S3`],design:[n(`Recovery and geographic separation are different needs. Versioning addresses recovering from deletions and overwrites, while S3 replication to a bucket in another Region, or an AWS Backup copy to another Region, keeps a copy away from the source. AWS says cross-Region backup is particularly valuable for business continuity or compliance requirements to store backups a minimum distance away from production data.`,[`s3-versioning`,`backup-what-is`,`s3-replication`],[`s3-versioning|Versioning-enabled buckets can help you recover objects from accidental deletion or overwrite.`,`backup-what-is|Cross-Region backup is particularly valuable if you have business continuity or compliance requirements to store backups a minimum distance away from your production data.`,`s3-replication|The destination buckets can be in different AWS Regions or within the same Region as the source bucket.`])]}],cues:[e(`recover an accidentally deleted or overwritten object`,`S3 Versioning`,n(`Versioning-enabled buckets can help you recover objects from accidental deletion or overwrite.`,`s3-versioning`,[`Versioning-enabled buckets can help you recover objects from accidental deletion or overwrite.`])),e(`centralize and automate backups across AWS services and accounts`,`AWS Backup`,n(`AWS Backup centralises and automates data protection across AWS services, and can manage backups across an organization.`,`backup-what-is`,[`AWS Backup is a fully-managed service that makes it easy to centralize and automate data protection across AWS services, in the cloud, and on premises.`])),e(`copy data to another Region for disaster recovery or compliance`,`S3 Cross-Region Replication or an AWS Backup cross-Region copy`,n(`S3 replication destinations can be in different Regions, and AWS Backup can copy backups across Regions.`,[`s3-replication`,`backup-what-is`],[`s3-replication|The destination buckets can be in different AWS Regions or within the same Region as the source bucket.`,`backup-what-is|Using AWS Backup, you can copy backups to multiple different AWS Regions on demand or automatically as part of a scheduled backup plan.`])),e(`discover where personally identifiable or sensitive data is stored in S3`,`Amazon Macie`,n(`Macie automates discovery, logging and reporting of sensitive data in your S3 data estate.`,`macie-discovery`,[`With Amazon Macie, you can automate discovery, logging, and reporting of sensitive data in your Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) data estate.`])),e(`objects must not be deleted or overwritten for a fixed retention period (WORM)`,`S3 Object Lock (needs versioning; compliance mode when even the root user must not delete)`,n(`S3 Object Lock can prevent objects from being deleted or overwritten for a fixed or variable amount of time, or indefinitely, and compliance mode applies even to the root user.`,`s3-object-lock`,[`S3 Object Lock can help prevent Amazon S3 objects from being deleted or overwritten for a fixed or variable amount of time, or indefinitely.`,`In compliance mode, a protected object version can't be overwritten or deleted by any user, including the root user in your AWS account.`]))],examples:[],confuse:[],azure:[{concept:`Azure Backup`,aws:`AWS Backup`,mapping:n(`Both are backup services. Microsoft Learn lists Azure Backup, which backs up and recovers files, databases, disks and virtual machines, against AWS Backup, which AWS describes as centralizing and automating data protection.`,[`learn-storage`,`backup-what-is`],[`learn-storage|This option is used to back up and recover files, databases, disks, and virtual machines.`,`backup-what-is|AWS Backup is a fully-managed service that makes it easy to centralize and automate data protection across AWS services, in the cloud, and on premises.`]),breaks:n(`The two descriptions emphasise different things. Learn says Azure Backup is used to back up and recover files, databases, disks and virtual machines, and that it also supports backing up compatible on-premises Windows systems. AWS describes AWS Backup as centralising and automating data protection across AWS services, in the cloud and on premises, with cross-Region copies and management across accounts in an organization. The Learn comparison does not mention cross-Region copies or organization-wide management.`,[`backup-what-is`,`learn-storage`],[`backup-what-is|Using AWS Backup, you can copy backups to multiple different AWS Regions on demand or automatically as part of a scheduled backup plan.`,`backup-what-is|You can use AWS Backup to manage your backups across all AWS accounts inside your AWS Organizations structure.`,`learn-storage|This option is used to back up and recover files, databases, disks, and virtual machines.`,`learn-storage|Azure Backup also supports backing up compatible on-premises Windows systems.`],{allow:[`two`]})}]},i={building:`certificate-office`,overview:[n(`Encryption in transit means traffic is protected while it moves between a client and a service, usually with TLS. TLS needs a certificate, and certificates and keys expire or age out. {{term:acm|ACM}} issues and manages public and private SSL/TLS certificates for integrated AWS services, and AWS KMS can rotate the keys that protect data at rest.`,[`acm-overview`,`kms-rotation`],[`acm-overview|AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) handles the complexity of creating, storing, and renewing public and private SSL/TLS X.509 certificates and keys that protect your AWS websites and applications.`,`kms-rotation|you can rotate the key material associated with an existing KMS key by enabling automatic key rotation or performing on-demand rotation.`])],beyondProject:[n(`If your CloudFront site uses your own domain name over HTTPS, it needs a certificate. AWS documents a specific rule for that: to use an ACM certificate with a CloudFront distribution, request or import it in the US East (N. Virginia) Region.`,`cf-https-requirements`,[`To use an ACM certificate with a CloudFront distribution, make sure you request (or import) the certificate in the US East (N`])],bullets:[{id:`1.3-S3`,concepts:[n(`ACM handles creating, storing and renewing public and private SSL/TLS X.509 certificates and keys that protect your AWS websites and applications. You can issue certificates in ACM or import third-party certificates, and a certificate can secure single names, several names or wildcard domains.`,`acm-overview`,[`AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) handles the complexity of creating, storing, and renewing public and private SSL/TLS X.509 certificates and keys that protect your AWS websites and applications.`,`You can provide certificates for your integrated AWS services either by issuing them directly with ACM or by importing third-party certificates into the ACM management system.`,`ACM certificates can secure singular domain names, multiple specific domain names, wildcard domains, or combinations of these.`]),n(`ACM certificates are used directly by integrated services such as Elastic Load Balancing and CloudFront. An HTTPS listener on an Application Load Balancer needs at least one server certificate, which the load balancer uses to terminate the front-end connection and decrypt client requests before sending them on.`,[`acm-services`,`alb-https-listener`],[`acm-services|ACM certificates are supported by the following services: Elastic Load Balancing`,`alb-https-listener|To create an HTTPS listener, you must deploy at least one SSL server certificate on your load balancer.`,`alb-https-listener|The load balancer uses a server certificate to terminate the front-end connection and then decrypt requests from clients before sending them to the targets.`]),n(`Application Load Balancer listeners support the HTTP and HTTPS protocols, not TCP. To pass encrypted traffic to targets without the load balancer decrypting it, AWS says to create a Network Load Balancer (or a Classic Load Balancer) with a TCP listener, which passes encrypted traffic through to the targets.`,[`alb-https-listener`,`alb-listeners`],[`alb-listeners|Listeners support the following protocols and ports: Protocols: HTTP, HTTPS`,`alb-https-listener|If you need to pass encrypted traffic to targets without the load balancer decrypting it, you can create a Network Load Balancer or Classic Load Balancer with a TCP listener on port 443.`,`alb-https-listener|With a TCP listener, the load balancer passes encrypted traffic through to the targets without decrypting it.`]),n(`An Application Load Balancer target group can also use the HTTPS protocol, so the connection between the load balancer and its targets can be HTTPS as well as the client connection.`,`alb-target-groups`,[`Protocols: HTTP, HTTPS`]),n(`Edge-optimized API Gateway custom domain names follow the same Region rule as CloudFront: API Gateway leverages CloudFront to support certificates for edge-optimized custom domain names, so an ACM certificate for one must be requested or imported in the US East (N. Virginia) us-east-1 Region.`,`apigw-edge-cert`,[`If you create an edge-optimized custom domain name, API Gateway leverages CloudFront to support certificates for custom domain names.`,`Virginia) – us-east-1 Region.`])],services:[`AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)`],design:[n(`To protect a public website: request a public certificate in ACM and attach it to the load balancer or CloudFront distribution. For CloudFront the certificate must be in the US East (N. Virginia) Region.`,[`acm-services`,`cf-https-requirements`],[`acm-services|ACM certificates are supported by the following services: Elastic Load Balancing`,`cf-https-requirements|To use an ACM certificate with a CloudFront distribution, make sure you request (or import) the certificate in the US East (N`])]},{id:`1.3-S7`,concepts:[n(`ACM provides managed renewal for Amazon-issued certificates. ACM renews them automatically if you use DNS validation, or sends email notices as expiration approaches. A certificate is eligible for automatic renewal if it is associated with another AWS service such as Elastic Load Balancing or CloudFront.`,`acm-renewal`,[`ACM provides managed renewal for your Amazon-issued SSL/TLS certificates.`,`ACM will either renew your certificates automatically (if you are using DNS validation), or it will send you email notices when expiration is approaching.`,`ELIGIBLE if associated with another AWS service, such as Elastic Load Balancing or CloudFront.`]),n(`For keys, AWS KMS can rotate key material. By default, enabling automatic key rotation on a KMS key makes AWS KMS generate new cryptographic material for it every year; you can also set a custom rotation period or rotate on demand.`,`kms-rotation`,[`By default, when you enable automatic key rotation for a KMS key, AWS KMS generates new cryptographic material for the KMS key every year.`,`You can also specify a custom rotation-period to define the number of days after you enable automatic key rotation that AWS KMS will rotate your key material`],{newer:[`kms-on-demand-rotation`]}),n(`Rotation facts differ by key type. AWS KMS automatically rotates AWS managed keys every year and you cannot enable or disable that rotation. Automatic rotation is optional for customer managed keys and is supported only on symmetric encryption KMS keys with key material that AWS KMS generates; asymmetric KMS keys, HMAC KMS keys and keys in custom key stores can only be rotated manually. Rotating the key material does not change the properties of the KMS key.`,`kms-rotation`,[`AWS KMS automatically rotates AWS managed keys every year (approximately 365 days). You cannot enable or disable key rotation for AWS managed keys.`,`Automatic key rotation is supported only on symmetric encryption KMS keys with key material that AWS KMS generates (AWS_KMS origin).`,`Automatic rotation is optional for customer managed KMS keys.`,`Neither automatic nor on-demand key rotation is supported for the following types of KMS keys, but you can rotate these KMS keys manually.`,`The properties of the KMS key do not change`]),n(`ACM renewal has limits too: a certificate you imported is not eligible for managed renewal.`,`acm-renewal`,[`NOT ELIGIBLE if imported.`]),n(`Secrets Manager can also rotate secrets automatically; see Warden's Lodge.`,`secrets-rotation`,[`In Secrets Manager, you can set up automatic rotation for your secrets.`])],services:[`AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)`,`AWS KMS`],design:[n(`To avoid outages from expired certificates, use ACM-issued certificates with DNS validation on integrated services so renewal is automatic. To meet a key-rotation requirement for encrypted data, enable automatic rotation on the customer managed KMS key rather than building your own process.`,[`acm-renewal`,`kms-rotation`],[`acm-renewal|ACM will either renew your certificates automatically (if you are using DNS validation), or it will send you email notices when expiration is approaching.`,`kms-rotation|Or, you can rotate the key material associated with an existing KMS key by enabling automatic key rotation or performing on-demand rotation.`])]}],cues:[e(`HTTPS for a website / manage SSL/TLS certificates with minimal effort`,`AWS Certificate Manager`,n(`ACM handles creating, storing and renewing SSL/TLS certificates and keys.`,`acm-overview`,[`AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) handles the complexity of creating, storing, and renewing public and private SSL/TLS X.509 certificates and keys`])),e(`certificates must renew automatically`,`ACM-issued certificate with DNS validation on an integrated service`,n(`ACM renews certificates automatically when DNS validation is used.`,`acm-renewal`,[`ACM will either renew your certificates automatically (if you are using DNS validation)`])),e(`CloudFront with a custom domain over HTTPS`,`ACM certificate in US East (N. Virginia)`,n(`For CloudFront, request or import the ACM certificate in US East (N. Virginia).`,`cf-https-requirements`,[`make sure you request (or import) the certificate in the US East (N`])),e(`rotate encryption keys annually`,`enable automatic key rotation on the KMS key`,n(`Enabling automatic key rotation makes KMS generate new key material every year by default.`,`kms-rotation`,[`AWS KMS generates new cryptographic material for the KMS key every year.`])),e(`traffic must remain encrypted all the way to the instance`,`a Network Load Balancer TCP listener (pass-through), or HTTPS from the load balancer to the targets`,n(`A Network Load Balancer (or Classic Load Balancer) TCP listener passes encrypted traffic through to targets without decrypting it; an Application Load Balancer target group can use HTTPS.`,[`alb-https-listener`,`alb-target-groups`],[`alb-https-listener|the load balancer passes encrypted traffic through to the targets without decrypting it`,`alb-target-groups|Protocols: HTTP, HTTPS`])),e(`certificates for a domain you imported yourself`,`ACM does not renew imported certificates`,n(`A certificate imported into ACM is not eligible for ACM managed renewal.`,`acm-renewal`,[`NOT ELIGIBLE if imported.`]))],examples:[],confuse:[],azure:[{concept:`Key Vault certificates`,aws:`AWS Certificate Manager`,mapping:n(`The Learn comparison pairs ACM with Key Vault certificates and Microsoft Cloud PKI as the services to create and manage certificates and their keys.`,[`learn-hub`,`acm-overview`],[`learn-hub|Use these services to create and manage certificates and their keys.`,`acm-overview|AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) handles the complexity of creating, storing, and renewing public and private SSL/TLS X.509 certificates and keys`]),breaks:n(`ACM certificates can be used directly by integrated AWS services such as Elastic Load Balancing and CloudFront, and a CloudFront certificate has to be in US East (N. Virginia). The ACM documentation also describes automating public certificate issuance and renewal outside integrated services with the ACME protocol, and exportable certificates; the Learn comparison lists Key Vault certificates and Microsoft Cloud PKI against ACM without describing either.`,[`acm-services`,`cf-https-requirements`,`learn-hub`],[`acm-services|ACM certificates are supported by the following services: Elastic Load Balancing`,`acm-services|To automate public certificate issuance and renewal outside integrated services such as on Amazon EC2 instances, use the ACME protocol.`,`acm-services|you can automate exportable certificates issued from ACM through AWS Workload Credentials Provider`,`cf-https-requirements|make sure you request (or import) the certificate in the US East (N`,`learn-hub|Key Vault certificates Microsoft Cloud PKI`])}]},a={building:`charter-hall`,overview:[n(`Two ideas sit under every other design decision. First, where your workload physically lives: a {{term:region}} and its {{term:availability-zone|Availability Zones}}. Second, who is responsible for what once it is running: the {{term:shared-responsibility-model}}. Get these wrong and both resilience and security answers fall apart.`,[`global-infra`,`shared-responsibility`],[`global-infra|AWS has the concept of a Region, which is a physical location around the world where we cluster data centers.`,`shared-responsibility|Customer responsibility “Security in the Cloud” Customer responsibility will be determined by the AWS Cloud services that a customer selects.`],{allow:[`two`]})],bullets:[{id:`1.1-K3`,concepts:[n(`A Region is a physical location in the world where AWS clusters data centers, and each group of logical data centers is an Availability Zone. An Availability Zone is one or more discrete data centers with separate and redundant power, networking and connectivity in a Region.`,[`global-infra`,`fault-isolation-az`],[`global-infra|AWS has the concept of a Region, which is a physical location around the world where we cluster data centers.`,`global-infra|We call each group of logical data centers an Availability Zone.`,`fault-isolation-az|An Availability Zone is one or more discrete data centers with separate and redundant power infrastructure, networking, and connectivity in an AWS Region.`]),n(`Zones in a Region are meaningfully distant from each other, up to 60 miles (~100 km) to prevent correlated failures, yet close enough for synchronous replication with single-digit millisecond latency. Common points of failure such as generators and cooling equipment are not shared across zones.`,`fault-isolation-az`,[`Availability Zones in a Region are meaningfully distant from each other, up to 60 miles (~100 km) to prevent correlated failures, but close enough to use synchronous replication with single-digit millisecond latency.`,`Common points of failure, like generators and cooling equipment, are not shared across Availability Zones`]),n(`When you choose a Region, consider which one has the services and features you need, which one is close to most of your users to lower latency, and whether legal or regulatory requirements apply, for example keeping resources in Europe to be closer to European customers or to meet legal requirements. Your account determines which Regions are available to you.`,`regions-and-azs`,[`select a Region that has the AWS services and features that you need.`,`you can lower network latency when you select a Region that is close to the majority of your users.`,`For example, you want to create resources in Europe to be closer to your European customers or to meet legal requirements.`,`This information can help you meet your regulatory, compliance, and operational requirements.`,`Your account determines the Regions that are available to you.`])],services:[],design:[n(`Spread tiers across more than one Availability Zone to survive the loss of a zone, and choose the Region by service availability, user latency and any legal or regulatory requirements. AWS describes each Region as consisting of at least three independent, physically separate Availability Zones, so three is a minimum rather than a fixed number.`,[`global-infra-overview`,`global-infra`],[`global-infra-overview|With each AWS Region consisting of at least three independent, physically separate Availability Zones (AZs), AWS delivers the highest availability and reliability for your applications.`,`global-infra|Each AWS Region consists of a minimum of three, isolated, and physically separate AZs within a geographic area.`])]},{id:`1.1-K5`,concepts:[n(`AWS is responsible for "Security of the Cloud": protecting the infrastructure that runs all of the services offered in the AWS Cloud. The customer is responsible for "Security in the Cloud", and what that includes is determined by the AWS services the customer selects.`,`shared-responsibility`,[`AWS responsibility “Security of the Cloud” AWS is responsible for protecting the infrastructure that runs all of the services offered in the AWS Cloud.`,`Customer responsibility “Security in the Cloud” Customer responsibility will be determined by the AWS Cloud services that a customer selects.`]),n(`The model relieves operational burden because AWS operates and controls the components from the host operating system and virtualization layer down to the physical security of the facilities. For an infrastructure service such as EC2, the customer takes on the guest operating system (including updates and security patches), application software and the configuration of the security group firewall.`,`shared-responsibility`,[`This shared model can help relieve the customer’s operational burden as AWS operates, manages and controls the components from the host operating system and virtualization layer down to the physical security of the facilities in which the service operates.`,`The customer assumes responsibility and management of the guest operating system (including updates and security patches), other associated application software as well as the configuration of the AWS provided security group firewall.`])],services:[],design:[n(`Use the model to read "who patches this?" questions: the customer's responsibility is determined by the AWS services selected, so ask for each service which parts AWS operates and which the customer still owns.`,`shared-responsibility`,[`Customer responsibility will be determined by the AWS Cloud services that a customer selects.`])]}],cues:[e(`survive the failure of a data center / one Availability Zone`,`deploy across multiple Availability Zones`,n(`Availability Zones have separate and redundant power, networking and connectivity within a Region.`,`fault-isolation-az`,[`separate and redundant power infrastructure, networking, and connectivity`])),e(`lowest latency for users in a geography`,`choose the Region closest to them`,n(`Selecting a Region close to the majority of your users lowers network latency.`,`regions-and-azs`,[`you can lower network latency when you select a Region that is close to the majority of your users.`])),e(`who is responsible for patching the guest operating system`,`the customer, for infrastructure services such as EC2`,n(`For EC2-style infrastructure services the customer assumes responsibility for the guest operating system, including updates and security patches.`,`shared-responsibility`,[`The customer assumes responsibility and management of the guest operating system (including updates and security patches)`])),e(`physical security of the data centers`,`AWS`,n(`AWS operates and controls components down to the physical security of the facilities in which the service operates.`,`shared-responsibility`,[`down to the physical security of the facilities in which the service operates`]))],examples:[],confuse:[],azure:[{concept:`Azure regions and availability zones`,aws:`AWS Regions and Availability Zones`,mapping:n(`The model is the same. Azure describes an availability zone as a logical grouping of one or more physically separate datacenters within a region; AWS describes an Availability Zone as one or more discrete data centers with separate power, networking and connectivity in a Region.`,[`learn-zones`,`fault-isolation-az`],[`learn-zones|An availability zone is a logical grouping of one or more physically separate datacenters within a region.`,`fault-isolation-az|An Availability Zone is one or more discrete data centers with separate and redundant power infrastructure, networking, and connectivity in an AWS Region.`]),breaks:n(`Learn says that many Azure regions provide availability zones, which implies that not all do. AWS describes each Region as consisting of a minimum of three isolated, physically separate Availability Zones. The Regions available to you also depend on your account.`,[`learn-zones`,`global-infra`,`regions-and-azs`],[`learn-zones|Many Azure regions provide availability zones, which are separated groups of datacenters within a region.`,`global-infra|Each AWS Region consists of a minimum of three, isolated, and physically separate AZs within a geographic area.`,`regions-and-azs|Your account determines the Regions that are available to you.`],{allow:[`three`]})},{concept:`Shared responsibility in the cloud`,aws:`AWS shared responsibility model`,mapping:n(`Both clouds use a shared responsibility model. Azure explains that workload responsibilities vary depending on whether the workload is SaaS, PaaS, IaaS or on-premises; AWS says customer responsibility is determined by the AWS services a customer selects.`,[`learn-shared-responsibility`,`shared-responsibility`],[`learn-shared-responsibility|Workload responsibilities vary depending on whether the workload is hosted on software as a service (SaaS), platform as a service (PaaS), infrastructure as a service (IaaS), or in an on-premises datacenter`,`shared-responsibility|Customer responsibility will be determined by the AWS Cloud services that a customer selects.`]),breaks:n(`The vocabulary differs: AWS frames it as security "of" the cloud (AWS) versus "in" the cloud (customer), and says the customer's part is determined by the AWS services selected. Learn describes the split by service model: SaaS, PaaS, IaaS or on-premises.`,[`learn-shared-responsibility`,`shared-responsibility`],[`learn-shared-responsibility|IaaS (infrastructure as a service): You manage virtual machines, operating systems, and applications.`,`shared-responsibility|AWS responsibility “Security of the Cloud” AWS is responsible for protecting the infrastructure that runs all of the services offered in the AWS Cloud.`])}]},o={building:`compliance-registry`,overview:[n(`Compliance questions ask you to prove or enforce that data and resources meet a rule. AWS gives you evidence (compliance documents in AWS Artifact), continuous checking (AWS Config rules) and data-level governance (Lake Formation). The concepts below add S3 settings that protect data.`,[`artifact`,`config-rules`,`lake-formation`],[`artifact|AWS Artifact provides on-demand downloads of AWS security and compliance documents.`,`config-rules|Use AWS Config to evaluate the configuration settings of your AWS resources.`,`lake-formation|AWS Lake Formation helps you centrally govern, secure, and globally share data for analytics and machine learning.`])],bullets:[{id:`1.3-K1`,concepts:[n(`Governing access to data means deciding who sees what, centrally. AWS Lake Formation helps you centrally govern, secure and share data for analytics and machine learning, with its own permissions model that augments IAM and fine-grained controls at the column, row and cell level.`,`lake-formation`,[`AWS Lake Formation helps you centrally govern, secure, and globally share data for analytics and machine learning.`,`Lake Formation provides its own permissions model that augments the IAM permissions model.`,`Lake Formation permissions are enforced using granular controls at the column, row, and cell-levels`]),n(`For S3, bucket policies, access point policies and object permissions can allow public access, and S3 Block Public Access settings override those policies and permissions. New buckets, access points and objects do not allow public access by default.`,`s3-block-public-access`,[`However, users can modify bucket policies, access point policies, or object permissions to allow public access.`,`By default, new buckets, access points, and objects don't allow public access.`,`S3 Block Public Access settings override these policies and permissions so that you can limit public access to these resources.`])],services:[`AWS Lake Formation`,`Amazon S3`,`Amazon Macie`],design:[n(`Data lake governed centrally by column, row or cell: Lake Formation. Object storage that should not be public: S3 Block Public Access, with Macie generating a finding if a bucket becomes publicly accessible.`,[`lake-formation`,`s3-block-public-access`,`macie`],[`lake-formation|With Lake Formation, you can manage fine-grained access control for your data lake data on Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) and its metadata in AWS Glue Data Catalog.`,`s3-block-public-access|S3 Block Public Access settings override these policies and permissions so that you can limit public access to these resources.`,`macie|such as a bucket that becomes publicly accessible`])]},{id:`1.3-S1`,concepts:[n(`AWS Artifact provides on-demand downloads of AWS security and compliance documents and certifications from accreditation bodies, and lets you review, accept and track the status of your agreements with AWS for one or many accounts.`,`artifact`,[`AWS Artifact provides on-demand downloads of AWS security and compliance documents.`,`AWS Artifact also provides downloads of certifications from accreditation bodies that validate the implementation and operating effectiveness of AWS security controls.`,`you can use AWS Artifact to review, accept, and track the status of your agreements with AWS for your AWS account and for multiple AWS accounts in your organization.`]),n(`AWS Config evaluates the configuration of your resources against {{term:config-rule|Config rules}}, and a conformance pack is a collection of Config rules and remediation actions that can be deployed as a single entity in an account and Region or across an organization.`,[`config-rules`,`config-conformance-packs`],[`config-rules|You do this by creating AWS Config rules, which represent your ideal configuration settings.`,`config-conformance-packs|A conformance pack is a collection of AWS Config rules and remediation actions that can be easily deployed as a single entity in an account and a Region or across an organization in AWS Organizations.`]),n(`CloudTrail provides the audit trail of API activity that compliance reviews ask for: actions by users, roles and services are recorded as events.`,`cloudtrail-what-is`,[`Actions taken by a user, role, or an AWS service are recorded as events in CloudTrail.`])],services:[`AWS Artifact`,`AWS Config`,`Amazon Macie`],design:[n(`To show an auditor AWS's own compliance status, download the reports from AWS Artifact. To show your own resources stay compliant, use Config rules or conformance packs and keep CloudTrail enabled for the audit trail.`,[`artifact`,`config-conformance-packs`,`cloudtrail-what-is`],[`artifact|AWS Artifact provides on-demand downloads of AWS security and compliance documents.`,`config-conformance-packs|A conformance pack is a collection of AWS Config rules and remediation actions`,`cloudtrail-what-is|Actions taken by a user, role, or an AWS service are recorded as events in CloudTrail.`])]},{id:`1.3-S6`,concepts:[n(`Public access: S3 Block Public Access settings override policies and permissions that allow public access. Lifecycle: an S3 Lifecycle configuration is a set of rules that define actions S3 applies to a group of objects, such as transitioning them to another storage class or deleting expired objects. Protection: S3 Object Lock uses a write-once-read-many model to help meet regulatory requirements, and it works only in buckets that have S3 Versioning enabled.`,[`s3-lifecycle`,`s3-object-lock`,`s3-block-public-access`],[`s3-block-public-access|S3 Block Public Access settings override these policies and permissions so that you can limit public access to these resources.`,`s3-lifecycle|An S3 Lifecycle configuration is a set of rules that define actions that Amazon S3 applies to a group of objects.`,`s3-lifecycle|Transition actions – These actions define when objects transition to another storage class.`,`s3-object-lock|You can use Object Lock to help meet regulatory requirements that require WORM storage, or to add another layer of protection against object changes or deletion.`,`s3-object-lock|Object Lock works only in buckets that have S3 Versioning enabled.`]),n(`Object Lock offers two ways to manage object retention: retention periods and legal holds. Its retention modes differ: in compliance mode no user, including the root user, can overwrite or delete a protected object version, whereas in governance mode users with special permissions can. AWS Backup Vault Lock also has governance and compliance modes, for backup vaults.`,[`s3-object-lock`,`backup-vault-lock`],[`s3-object-lock|Object Lock provides two ways to manage object retention: retention periods and legal holds.`,`s3-object-lock|In compliance mode, a protected object version can't be overwritten or deleted by any user, including the root user in your AWS account.`,`s3-object-lock|In governance mode, users can't overwrite or delete an object version or alter its lock settings unless they have special permissions.`,`backup-vault-lock|When you create a vault lock, you have a choice of two modes: Governance mode or Compliance mode.`],{allow:[`two`]})],services:[`Amazon S3`,`AWS Backup`,`IAM`],design:[n(`Regulated data that must not be altered: Object Lock (WORM) on the bucket. Data that should move to lower-cost storage or expire: a lifecycle configuration (covered in depth at the Cold Cellar). Data that should not be exposed publicly: Block Public Access.`,[`s3-object-lock`,`s3-lifecycle`,`s3-block-public-access`],[`s3-object-lock|Object Lock uses a write-once-read-many (WORM) model to store objects.`,`s3-lifecycle|S3 Lifecycle helps you store objects cost effectively throughout their lifecycle by transitioning them to lower-cost storage classes, or, deleting expired objects on your behalf.`,`s3-block-public-access|By default, new buckets, access points, and objects don't allow public access.`])]}],cues:[e(`download AWS compliance reports / audit documents`,`AWS Artifact`,n(`AWS Artifact provides on-demand downloads of AWS security and compliance documents.`,`artifact`,[`AWS Artifact provides on-demand downloads of AWS security and compliance documents.`])),e(`continuously evaluate resource configuration against rules`,`AWS Config rules / conformance packs`,n(`AWS Config evaluates configuration settings of resources against rules.`,`config-rules`,[`Use AWS Config to evaluate the configuration settings of your AWS resources.`])),e(`WORM / data must not be modified or deleted for a retention period`,`S3 Object Lock (versioning required; compliance mode is the one that holds against the root user)`,n(`Object Lock uses a write-once-read-many model, can help meet regulatory requirements that require WORM storage, and works only in buckets that have S3 Versioning enabled.`,`s3-object-lock`,[`You can use Object Lock to help meet regulatory requirements that require WORM storage`,`Object Lock works only in buckets that have S3 Versioning enabled.`])),e(`prevent public access to buckets`,`S3 Block Public Access`,n(`S3 Block Public Access settings override bucket policies and permissions that would allow public access.`,`s3-block-public-access`,[`S3 Block Public Access settings override these policies and permissions so that you can limit public access to these resources.`])),e(`fine-grained (column / row) access control over a data lake`,`AWS Lake Formation`,n(`Lake Formation enforces granular controls at the column, row and cell levels.`,`lake-formation`,[`Lake Formation permissions are enforced using granular controls at the column, row, and cell-levels`]))],examples:[],confuse:[],azure:[{concept:`Microsoft Service Trust Portal`,aws:`AWS Artifact`,mapping:n(`Both give customers access to audit reports, compliance guides and trust documents from the cloud provider.`,[`learn-hub`,`artifact`],[`learn-hub|Use these services to access audit reports, compliance guides, and trust documents from across cloud services.`,`artifact|AWS Artifact provides on-demand downloads of AWS security and compliance documents.`]),breaks:n(`AWS Artifact also lets you review, accept and track the status of your agreements with AWS for one account or many accounts in your organization. The Learn comparison describes the pair only as services to access audit reports, compliance guides and trust documents; it does not mention agreements.`,[`artifact`,`learn-hub`],[`artifact|you can use AWS Artifact to review, accept, and track the status of your agreements with AWS for your AWS account and for multiple AWS accounts in your organization.`,`learn-hub|Use these services to access audit reports, compliance guides, and trust documents from across cloud services.`])},{concept:`Azure Policy`,aws:`AWS Config`,mapping:n(`The Learn comparison pairs AWS Config with Azure Policy and Change Analysis: governance for consistency and compliance of resources.`,[`learn-hub`,`config-rules`],[`learn-hub|Azure Policy helps you implement governance for resource consistency, regulatory compliance, security, cost, and management.`,`config-rules|Use AWS Config to evaluate the configuration settings of your AWS resources.`]),breaks:n(`Learn describes Azure Policy as helping you implement governance, with bulk remediation for existing resources and automatic remediation for new resources, and says you typically use AWS Config to monitor for configuration changes or to identify and remediate noncompliant resources. AWS Config records and evaluates configuration against rules; the AWS tool that sets a ceiling on permissions across accounts is a different service, service control policies in AWS Organizations.`,[`config-rules`,`orgs-scps`,`learn-hub`],[`config-rules|You do this by creating AWS Config rules, which represent your ideal configuration settings.`,`orgs-scps|SCPs offer central control over the maximum available permissions for the IAM users and IAM roles in your organization.`,`learn-hub|Use Azure Policy for bulk remediation for existing resources and automatic remediation for new resources.`,`learn-hub|You typically use AWS Config to monitor for configuration changes or to identify and remediate noncompliant resources.`])}]},s={building:`customs-house`,overview:[n(`Traffic crosses the VPC boundary in three ways, and each has a secure option: calls to AWS service {{term:service-endpoint|service endpoints}} (which can be made private with VPC endpoints), connections from your on-premises network (Site-to-Site VPN, Direct Connect), and remote users (Client VPN). This building is about choosing the one that keeps the traffic where you want it.`,[`service-endpoints`,`vpce-gateway`,`s2s-vpn`,`client-vpn`],[`service-endpoints|An endpoint is the URL of the entry point for an AWS web service.`,`vpce-gateway|Gateway VPC endpoints provide reliable connectivity to Amazon S3 and DynamoDB without requiring an internet gateway or a NAT device for your VPC.`,`s2s-vpn|You can enable access to your remote devices from your VPC by creating an AWS Site-to-Site VPN (Site-to-Site VPN) connection`,`client-vpn|AWS Client VPN is a managed client-based VPN service that enables you to securely access your AWS resources and resources in your on-premises network.`],{allow:[`three`]})],bullets:[{id:`1.2-K2`,concepts:[n(`An AWS service endpoint is the URL of the entry point for an AWS web service. The SDKs and CLI use the default endpoint for each service in a Region, and you can specify an alternate endpoint, such as a FIPS or dual-stack endpoint, for your API requests.`,`service-endpoints`,[`An endpoint is the URL of the entry point for an AWS web service.`,`The AWS SDKs and the AWS Command Line Interface (AWS CLI) automatically use the default endpoint for each service in an AWS Region.`,`But you can specify an alternate endpoint for your API requests.`]),n(`Some services can be reached either through their public service endpoints or privately, through a {{term:vpc-endpoint}}. Amazon S3 and DynamoDB support both gateway endpoints and interface endpoints. Interface endpoints are powered by AWS PrivateLink, which lets you privately connect your VPC to services and resources as if they were in your VPC; gateway endpoints do not use PrivateLink.`,[`vpce-gateway`,`vpce-interface`,`privatelink-what-is`],[`vpce-gateway|You can access Amazon S3 and DynamoDB through their public service endpoints or through gateway endpoints.`,`vpce-gateway|Amazon S3 and DynamoDB support both gateway endpoints and interface endpoints.`,`vpce-gateway|Gateway endpoints do not use AWS PrivateLink, unlike other types of VPC endpoints.`,`vpce-interface|You can create an interface VPC endpoint to connect to services powered by AWS PrivateLink, including many AWS services.`,`privatelink-what-is|AWS PrivateLink is a highly available, scalable technology that you can use to privately connect your VPC to services and resources as if they were in your VPC.`]),n(`Gateway endpoints cover Amazon S3 and DynamoDB and are targets in a route table, while interface endpoints put an endpoint network interface with a private IP address in each subnet you choose. Compare them side by side in the Don't confuse section below.`,[`vpce-gateway`,`vpce-interface`],[`vpce-gateway|Gateway VPC endpoints provide reliable connectivity to Amazon S3 and DynamoDB without requiring an internet gateway or a NAT device for your VPC.`,`vpce-interface|For each subnet that you specify from your VPC, we create an endpoint network interface in the subnet and assign it a private IP address from the subnet address range.`])],services:[`Amazon VPC`,`AWS PrivateLink`],design:[n(`Private subnets that only need S3 or DynamoDB do not need a NAT gateway for them: a gateway endpoint reaches those services without an internet gateway or NAT device. An interface endpoint reaches services powered by AWS PrivateLink, including many AWS services, and it is also the option for S3 when the access comes from on-premises networks, from peered VPCs in other Regions or through a transit gateway.`,[`vpce-gateway-s3`,`privatelink-concepts`,`vpce-gateway`,`vpce-interface`],[`vpce-gateway|Gateway VPC endpoints provide reliable connectivity to Amazon S3 and DynamoDB without requiring an internet gateway or a NAT device for your VPC.`,`vpce-interface|You can create an interface VPC endpoint to connect to services powered by AWS PrivateLink, including many AWS services.`,`vpce-gateway-s3|With a gateway endpoint, you can access Amazon S3 from your VPC, without requiring an internet gateway or NAT device for your VPC, and with no additional cost.`,`vpce-gateway-s3|However, gateway endpoints do not allow access from on-premises networks, from peered VPCs in other AWS Regions, or through a transit gateway.`,`vpce-gateway-s3|For those scenarios, you must use an interface endpoint, which is available for an additional cost.`,`privatelink-concepts|Consumers create VPC endpoints to connect to endpoint services and resources that are hosted by providers.`])]},{id:`1.2-S4`,concepts:[n(`AWS Site-to-Site VPN creates an IPsec VPN connection between your on-premises network and your VPC. Each VPN connection includes two tunnels you can use simultaneously for high availability. It terminates on a virtual private gateway or a transit gateway, with a customer gateway describing your device.`,[`s2s-vpn`,`s2s-vpn-how`],[`s2s-vpn|Site-to-Site VPN supports Internet Protocol security (IPsec) VPN connections.`,`s2s-vpn|Each VPN connection includes two VPN tunnels which you can simultaneously use for high availability.`,`s2s-vpn-how|Transit gateway A transit gateway is a transit hub that you can use to interconnect your VPCs and your on-premises networks.`]),n(`Direct Connect links your network to a Direct Connect location over a fiber-optic cable, bypassing internet service providers in your network path. AWS says it can reduce network costs, increase bandwidth and provide a more consistent experience than internet-based connections. A private path is not the same as an encrypted one: AWS Direct Connect does not encrypt your traffic in transit by default, and combining it with Site-to-Site VPN provides an IPsec-encrypted private connection.`,[`dx-what-is`,`connectivity-dx`,`dx-encryption`],[`dx-what-is|bypassing internet service providers in your network path.`,`dx-encryption|AWS Direct Connect does not encrypt your traffic that is in transit by default.`,`dx-encryption|This combination provides an IPsec-encrypted private connection`,`connectivity-dx|Direct Connect can reduce network costs, increase bandwidth throughput, and provide a more consistent network experience than internet-based connections.`]),n(`AWS Client VPN is a managed client-based VPN service for individual remote users; it uses an OpenVPN-based client and establishes encrypted TLS connections.`,`client-vpn`,[`AWS Client VPN is a managed client-based VPN service that enables you to securely access your AWS resources and resources in your on-premises network.`,`With Client VPN, you can access your resources from any location using an OpenVPN-based VPN client.`,`Establishes encrypted TLS connections from any location through the OpenVPN client`])],services:[`AWS Site-to-Site VPN`,`AWS Direct Connect`,`AWS Client VPN`],design:[n(`Site-to-site office or data-center connectivity over the internet with encryption: Site-to-Site VPN. A dedicated, consistent link: Direct Connect. Individual remote workers: Client VPN. Direct Connect and VPN are compared in Transit Tariff.`,[`connectivity-vpn`,`connectivity-dx`,`client-vpn`],[`connectivity-vpn|Amazon VPC provides the option of creating an IPsec VPN connection between your remote networks and Amazon VPC over the internet`,`connectivity-dx|AWS Direct Connect makes it easy to establish a dedicated connection from an on-premises network to one or more VPCs.`,`client-vpn|Managed service — Eliminates the operational burden of deploying and maintaining third-party remote access VPN solutions through complete AWS management.`])]}],cues:[e(`access Amazon S3 from private subnets without an internet gateway or NAT, at no extra cost`,`S3 gateway endpoint`,n(`With a gateway endpoint you can access S3 from your VPC without an internet gateway or NAT device and with no additional cost.`,`vpce-gateway-s3`,[`With a gateway endpoint, you can access Amazon S3 from your VPC, without requiring an internet gateway or NAT device for your VPC, and with no additional cost.`])),e(`privately access an AWS service or a SaaS product over private IP addresses`,`interface endpoint (AWS PrivateLink)`,n(`PrivateLink privately connects your VPC to services and resources as if they were in your VPC.`,`privatelink-what-is`,[`AWS PrivateLink is a highly available, scalable technology that you can use to privately connect your VPC to services and resources as if they were in your VPC.`])),e(`encrypted connection between the corporate data center and the VPC over the internet`,`AWS Site-to-Site VPN`,n(`Site-to-Site VPN is an IPsec VPN connection between your on-premises network and your VPC.`,`s2s-vpn`,[`Site-to-Site VPN supports Internet Protocol security (IPsec) VPN connections.`])),e(`dedicated private connection that does not use the public internet`,`AWS Direct Connect (not encrypted by default; add VPN for IPsec)`,n(`Direct Connect bypasses internet service providers in your network path, but it does not encrypt your traffic in transit by default.`,[`dx-what-is`,`dx-encryption`],[`dx-what-is|bypassing internet service providers in your network path.`,`dx-encryption|AWS Direct Connect does not encrypt your traffic that is in transit by default.`])),e(`remote employees connect securely from anywhere`,`AWS Client VPN`,n(`Client VPN lets you access resources from any location using an OpenVPN-based VPN client.`,`client-vpn`,[`With Client VPN, you can access your resources from any location using an OpenVPN-based VPN client.`]))],examples:[],confuse:[`gateway-vs-interface-endpoints`,`direct-connect-vs-vpn`],azure:[{concept:`VPN Gateway`,aws:`AWS Site-to-Site VPN`,mapping:n(`Microsoft Learn lists VPN Gateway as the Azure counterpart to Site-to-Site VPN, and describes both as robust solutions for connecting on-premises networks to the cloud.`,[`learn-networking`,`s2s-vpn`],[`learn-networking|Site-to-Site VPN VPN Gateway`,`learn-networking|are robust solutions for connecting on-premises networks to the cloud.`,`s2s-vpn|You can enable access to your remote devices from your VPC by creating an AWS Site-to-Site VPN (Site-to-Site VPN) connection`]),breaks:n(`Learn says the two services provide similar features but with a notable difference in performance, and lists route-based and policy-based VPNs as a feature difference on the Azure side. On the AWS side a Site-to-Site VPN connection consists of a virtual private gateway or a transit gateway, a customer gateway device and a customer gateway, and each connection includes two tunnels.`,[`s2s-vpn`,`s2s-vpn-how`,`learn-networking`],[`learn-networking|They provide similar features, but there's a notable difference with performance.`,`learn-networking|The key differences are in their integration with other cloud services and in specific features like route-based and policy-based VPNs in Azure.`,`s2s-vpn-how|A Site-to-Site VPN connection consists of the following components: A virtual private gateway or a transit gateway A customer gateway device A customer gateway`,`s2s-vpn|Each VPN connection includes two VPN tunnels which you can simultaneously use for high availability.`],{allow:[`two`]})},{concept:`ExpressRoute`,aws:`AWS Direct Connect`,mapping:n(`ExpressRoute and Direct Connect are the dedicated private-connection services; Microsoft Learn pairs them.`,[`learn-networking`,`dx-what-is`],[`learn-networking|AWS Direct Connect can link a network directly to AWS.`,`learn-networking|Azure provides similar site-to-site dedicated connections through ExpressRoute.`,`dx-what-is|Direct Connect links your internal network to a Direct Connect location over a standard Ethernet fiber-optic cable.`]),breaks:n(`With Direct Connect you create virtual interfaces to public AWS services or to a VPC, bypassing internet service providers in your network path, and AWS states that Direct Connect does not encrypt traffic in transit by default. Learn describes ExpressRoute as a dedicated private network connection and the comparison does not mention encryption for it.`,[`dx-what-is`,`dx-encryption`,`learn-networking`],[`dx-what-is|you can create virtual interfaces directly to public AWS services (for example, to Amazon S3) or to Amazon VPC, bypassing internet service providers in your network path.`,`dx-encryption|AWS Direct Connect does not encrypt your traffic that is in transit by default.`,`learn-networking|You can use ExpressRoute to connect your local network directly to Azure resources by using a dedicated private network connection.`])},{concept:`Azure Private Link`,aws:`AWS PrivateLink`,mapping:n(`Learn says Azure Private Link is similar to AWS PrivateLink: Private Link provides private connectivity from a virtual network to an Azure platform as a service (PaaS) solution, a customer-owned service, or a Microsoft partner service.`,[`learn-networking`,`privatelink-what-is`],[`learn-networking|Private Link is similar to AWS PrivateLink.`,`learn-networking|Azure Private Link provides private connectivity from a virtual network to an Azure platform as a service (PaaS) solution, a customer-owned service, or a Microsoft partner service.`,`privatelink-what-is|AWS PrivateLink is a highly available, scalable technology that you can use to privately connect your VPC to services and resources as if they were in your VPC.`]),breaks:n(`Learn lists AWS VPC endpoints against Azure Private Endpoint, and AWS PrivateLink against Azure Private Link. In AWS, "VPC endpoint" covers more than one type: a gateway endpoint sends traffic to Amazon S3 or DynamoDB and does not use AWS PrivateLink, unlike the other types of VPC endpoints.`,[`privatelink-concepts`,`learn-networking`],[`privatelink-concepts|There is another type of VPC endpoint, Gateway, which creates a gateway endpoint to send traffic to Amazon S3 or DynamoDB.`,`privatelink-concepts|Gateway endpoints do not use AWS PrivateLink, unlike the other types of VPC endpoints.`,`learn-networking|VPC endpoints Private Endpoint`])}]},c={building:`embassy-row`,overview:[n(`AWS recommends account-level separation for isolating production workloads from development and test workloads, so this building is about keeping a multi-account estate safe: grouping accounts under {{term:aws-organizations}}, limiting what they may do with {{term:scp|SCPs}}, setting up a governed multi-account environment with {{term:control-tower}}, and letting principals in one account use resources in another through roles or resource policies.`,[`orgs-intro`,`orgs-scps`,`control-tower`,`sec-account-separation`,`iam-cross-account`,`iam-account-switch`],[`orgs-intro|AWS Organizations helps you centrally manage and govern your environment as you grow and scale your AWS resources.`,`orgs-scps|SCPs offer central control over the maximum available permissions for the IAM users and IAM roles in your organization.`,`control-tower|AWS Control Tower offers a straightforward way to set up and govern an AWS multi-account environment, following prescriptive best practices.`,`sec-account-separation|For example, account-level separation is strongly recommended for isolating production workloads from development and test workloads.`,`iam-cross-account|To do this, you can attach a resource policy directly to the resource that you want to share, or use a role as a proxy.`,`iam-account-switch|A user in one account can switch to a role in the same or a different account.`])],beyondProject:[n(`If your CloudFront and S3 static site uses origin access control (OAC), an S3 bucket policy allows the CloudFront service principal to read the bucket, and that bucket policy is what SAA calls a resource policy for an AWS service. AWS says CloudFront can send authenticated requests to an S3 origin with either OAC or the older origin access identity (OAI), and recommends OAC. OAC does not apply if the origin is a bucket configured as a website endpoint. What SAA adds is choosing between a resource policy, a role and an SCP guardrail, and knowing what each can and cannot do.`,`cf-oac`,[`Use an S3 bucket policy to allow the CloudFront service principal (cloudfront.amazonaws.com) to access the bucket.`,`CloudFront provides two ways to send authenticated requests to an Amazon S3 origin: origin access control (OAC) and origin access identity (OAI).`,`We recommend that you use OAC instead because it supports the following features:`,`This origin must be a regular S3 bucket, not a bucket configured as a website endpoint.`],{newer:[`cloudfront-oac`],allow:[`two`]})],bullets:[{id:`1.1-K1`,concepts:[n(`AWS Organizations lets you create accounts, group them, apply policies for governance, and simplify billing with a single payment method for all of them. It integrates with other services to define central configurations, security mechanisms, audit requirements and resource sharing across accounts.`,`orgs-intro`,[`Using Organizations, you can create accounts and allocate resources, group accounts to organize your workflows, apply policies for governance, and simplify billing by using a single payment method for all of your accounts.`,`Organizations is integrated with other AWS services so you can define central configurations, security mechanisms, audit requirements, and resource sharing across accounts in your organization.`]),n(`Access across accounts comes in three flavours: a principal assumes a role in the other account, a resource-based policy names the outside principal directly, or {{term:aws-ram}} shares a resource with other accounts or an entire organization.`,[`iam-cross-account`,`ram-what-is`],[`iam-cross-account|To do this, you can attach a resource policy directly to the resource that you want to share, or use a role as a proxy.`,`ram-what-is|AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM) helps you securely share your resources across AWS accounts, within your organization or organizational units (OUs)`],{allow:[`three`]}),n(`With AWS RAM you create a resource once and make it usable by other accounts. You can share with every account in the organization, with specific organizational units, or with specific accounts by ID.`,`ram-what-is`,[`If you have multiple AWS accounts, you can create a resource once and use AWS RAM to make that resource usable by those other accounts.`,`You can also share with specific AWS accounts by account ID, regardless of whether the account is part of an organization.`])],services:[`AWS Organizations`,`AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM)`,`IAM`],design:[n(`AWS says accounts are a hard boundary, and recommends account-level separation to isolate production workloads from development and test workloads. Cross-account roles then make the allowed paths between accounts explicit, and can help prevent accidental changes to sensitive environments.`,[`sec-account-separation`,`iam-account-switch`],[`sec-account-separation|In AWS, accounts are a hard boundary.`,`sec-account-separation|For example, account-level separation is strongly recommended for isolating production workloads from development and test workloads.`,`iam-account-switch|With roles you can help prevent accidental changes to sensitive environments, especially if you combine them with auditing to help make sure that roles are only used when needed.`])]},{id:`1.1-S3`,concepts:[n(`{{term:sts}} issues temporary, short-term security credentials that are generated dynamically when requested. A role in another account is assumed through the AssumeRole operation after a trust relationship exists, and the administrator grants users or groups permission for the sts:AssumeRole action.`,[`iam-temp-creds`,`iam-cross-account-role`],[`iam-temp-creds|Temporary security credentials are not stored with the user but are generated dynamically and provided to the user when requested.`,`iam-cross-account-role|After you create the trust relationship, an IAM user or an application from the trusted account can use the AWS Security Token Service (AWS STS) AssumeRole API operation.`,`iam-cross-account-role|To do this, the administrator attaches a policy to the user or a group that grants permission for the sts:AssumeRole action.`]),n(`Role switching: you do not sign in to a role, but once signed in as an IAM user (or an Identity Center, SAML-federated or web-identity user) you can switch to one. That temporarily sets aside your original permissions and gives you the role's.`,`iam-switch-role`,[`However, you don't sign in to a role, but once signed in as an IAM user you can switch to an IAM role.`,`This temporarily sets aside your original user permissions and instead gives you the permissions assigned to the role.`]),n(`A cross-account role is defined by its trust policy: you specify the accounts whose users need access in the Principal element of the role's trust policy, and a user in one account can switch to a role in the same or a different account.`,`iam-account-switch`,[`you specify the accounts by ID whose users need access in the Principal element of the role's trust policy.`,`A user in one account can switch to a role in the same or a different account.`])],services:[`IAM`],design:[n(`Role-based access across accounts: when you need cross-account access, for example to promote an update from development to production, let users in one account switch to a role in the other account. The role's trust policy names the accounts whose users may assume it.`,[`iam-account-switch`,`iam-el-principal`],[`iam-account-switch|For example, you might need cross-account access when you are promoting an update from the development environment to the production environment.`,`iam-account-switch|A user in one account can switch to a role in the same or a different account.`,`iam-account-switch|you specify the accounts by ID whose users need access in the Principal element of the role's trust policy.`,`iam-el-principal|In IAM roles, use the Principal element in the role trust policy to specify who can assume the role.`])]},{id:`1.1-S4`,concepts:[n(`SCPs are a type of organization policy that offers central control over the maximum available permissions for IAM users and roles in the organization. They do not grant permissions, are available only when all features are enabled, and have no effect on users or roles in the management account. Effective permissions are the intersection of what the SCP allows and what identity-based and resource-based policies allow.`,`orgs-scps`,[`SCPs offer central control over the maximum available permissions for the IAM users and IAM roles in your organization.`,`SCPs do not grant permissions to the IAM users and IAM roles in your organization.`,`SCPs are available only in an organization that has all features enabled.`,`They have no effect on users or roles in the management account.`,`The effective permissions are the logical intersection between what is allowed by the SCP and resource control policies (RCPs) and what is allowed by the identity-based and resource-based policies.`]),n(`SCPs are attached to the organization root, to organizational units, or to accounts. Enabling SCPs attaches the AWS managed FullAWSAccess policy, which allows all services and actions; for a permission to be allowed in an account it must be allowed at each level above it.`,[`orgs-scp-eval`],[`This is why when you enable SCPs, AWS Organizations attaches an AWS managed SCP policy named FullAWSAccess which allows all services and actions.`,`For a permission or a service to be allowed at Account B, a SCP that allows the permission or service should be attached to Root, the Production OU, and to Account B itself.`]),n(`AWS Control Tower orchestrates AWS Organizations, AWS Service Catalog and AWS IAM Identity Center to build a landing zone, and applies controls (sometimes called guardrails) to keep accounts from drift. Controls can be preventive, detective or proactive and apply to a whole organizational unit.`,[`control-tower`,`control-tower-controls`],[`control-tower|AWS Control Tower orchestrates the capabilities of several other AWS services, including AWS Organizations, AWS Service Catalog, and AWS IAM Identity Center, to build a landing zone`,`control-tower|To help keep your organizations and accounts from drift, which is divergence from best practices, AWS Control Tower applies controls (sometimes called guardrails).`,`control-tower-controls|AWS Control Tower implements preventive, detective, and proactive controls that help you govern your resources and monitor compliance across groups of AWS accounts.`]),n(`SCP evaluation follows a deny-by-default model, so any permission not explicitly allowed in the SCPs is denied. A Deny in an SCP attached at any level on the path to an account denies that permission for the account, even if another SCP allows it.`,`orgs-scp-eval`,[`SCP evaluation follows a deny-by-default model, meaning that any permissions not explicitly allowed in the SCPs are denied.`,`For a permission to be denied for a specific account, any SCP from the root through each OU in the direct path to the account (including the target account itself) can deny that permission.`]),n(`Control Tower lets distributed teams provision new accounts quickly through configurable account templates in Account Factory.`,`control-tower`,[`AWS Control Tower enables end users on your distributed teams to provision new AWS accounts quickly, by means of configurable account templates in Account Factory.`])],services:[`AWS Organizations`,`AWS Control Tower`,`IAM`],design:[n(`Use Control Tower when the question wants a governed multi-account environment set up quickly with best practices. Use SCPs when the question wants a ceiling on what accounts can do. An SCP does not grant permissions on its own.`,[`control-tower`,`orgs-scps`],[`control-tower|AWS Control Tower offers a straightforward way to set up and govern an AWS multi-account environment, following prescriptive best practices.`,`orgs-scps|SCPs do not grant permissions to the IAM users and IAM roles in your organization.`])]},{id:`1.1-S5`,concepts:[n(`Resource-based policies are attached to a resource. Examples include S3 buckets, SQS queues, VPC endpoints, KMS keys and DynamoDB tables and streams. They must use the Principal element, which an identity-based policy cannot.`,[`iam-identity-vs-resource`,`iam-el-principal`],[`iam-identity-vs-resource|you can attach resource-based policies to Amazon S3 buckets, Amazon SQS queues, VPC endpoints, AWS Key Management Service encryption keys, Amazon DynamoDB tables and streams`,`iam-el-principal|You must use the Principal element in resource-based policies.`]),n(`For cross-account access, the resource-based policy in the trusting account names the principal of the trusted account, and the principal's own identity-based policy must also allow the request. Inside a single account the rule is looser: if either the identity-based policy or the resource-based policy allows the request and the other does not, the request is still allowed, provided no explicit deny applies. A grant in a resource-based policy to an IAM user ARN is not limited by an implicit deny in an identity-based policy or permissions boundary, and SCPs still set the maximum permissions for the IAM users and roles in an organization.`,[`iam-cross-account-eval`,`iam-policy-eval-basics`,`orgs-scps`,`iam-boundaries`],[`iam-cross-account-eval|To allow cross-account access, you attach a resource-based policy to the resource that you want to share. You must also attach an identity-based policy to the identity that acts as the principal in the request.`,`iam-policy-eval-basics|If either the identity-based policy or the resource-based policy within the same account allows the request and the other doesn't, the request is still allowed.`,`iam-policy-eval-basics|An explicit deny in any of the policies overrides the allow.`,`orgs-scps|SCPs offer central control over the maximum available permissions for the IAM users and IAM roles in your organization.`,`iam-boundaries|Within the same account, resource-based policies that grant permissions to an IAM user ARN (that is not an AWS STS federated user principal session) are not limited by an implicit deny in an identity-based policy or permissions boundary.`]),n(`AWS services themselves can be principals. A service principal such as cloudfront.amazonaws.com lets a service act on a resource, which is how a CloudFront distribution is allowed to read a private S3 origin.`,[`iam-el-principal`,`cf-oac`],[`iam-el-principal|You can specify any of the following principals in a policy: AWS account and root user IAM roles Role sessions IAM users Federated user principals AWS services All principals`,`cf-oac|Use an S3 bucket policy to allow the CloudFront service principal (cloudfront.amazonaws.com) to access the bucket.`])],services:[`IAM`,`Amazon S3`],design:[n(`Pick a resource policy when a resource must be shared with a specific outside principal and the resource supports resource-based policies. Check the Principal element carefully: whenever the trusted entity is another AWS account, any IAM principal can be granted access to the resource.`,`iam-cross-account`,[`To share the resource directly, the resource that you want to share must support resource-based policies.`,`Whenever the trusted entity is another AWS account, any IAM principal can be granted access to your resource.`])]}],cues:[e(`across multiple AWS accounts, centrally`,`AWS Organizations (and SCPs for guardrails)`,n(`AWS Organizations helps you centrally manage and govern your environment as you grow, applying policies across accounts.`,`orgs-intro`,[`AWS Organizations helps you centrally manage and govern your environment as you grow and scale your AWS resources.`])),e(`prevent any account in the OU from ... / maximum permissions`,`a service control policy`,n(`SCPs offer central control over the maximum available permissions for IAM users and roles in the organization.`,`orgs-scps`,[`SCPs offer central control over the maximum available permissions for the IAM users and IAM roles in your organization.`])),e(`quickly set up a secure multi-account environment with best practices`,`AWS Control Tower`,n(`Control Tower offers a straightforward way to set up and govern a multi-account environment following prescriptive best practices.`,`control-tower`,[`AWS Control Tower offers a straightforward way to set up and govern an AWS multi-account environment, following prescriptive best practices.`])),e(`grant access to users in another AWS account`,`a cross-account IAM role (or a resource-based policy)`,n(`To share across accounts, attach a resource policy to the resource or use a role as a proxy.`,`iam-cross-account`,[`To do this, you can attach a resource policy directly to the resource that you want to share, or use a role as a proxy.`])),e(`share a resource such as a subnet or license with other accounts without copying it`,`AWS Resource Access Manager`,n(`With AWS RAM you create a resource once and make it usable by other accounts.`,`ram-what-is`,[`you can create a resource once and use AWS RAM to make that resource usable by those other accounts.`])),e(`the CloudFront service needs to read a private S3 bucket`,`a bucket policy for the CloudFront service principal (origin access control)`,n(`An S3 bucket policy allows the CloudFront service principal to access the bucket.`,`cf-oac`,[`Use an S3 bucket policy to allow the CloudFront service principal (cloudfront.amazonaws.com) to access the bucket.`]))],examples:[t(`Service control policy that denies leaving the organization`,`resource-policy`,`
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "DenyLeavingTheOrganization",
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Action": "organizations:LeaveOrganization",
      "Resource": "*"
    }
  ]
}
`,[n("SCPs are similar to IAM permission policies and use almost the same syntax. This statement's Effect is Deny, so it blocks `organizations:LeaveOrganization` for the accounts it applies to.",[`orgs-scps`,`orgs-scp-eval`],[`orgs-scps|SCPs are similar to AWS Identity and Access Management permission policies and use almost the same syntax.`,`orgs-scp-eval|any SCP from the root through each OU in the direct path to the account (including the target account itself) can deny that permission`]),n(`The Deny does not depend on an Allow SCP. What the allow side decides is everything else: SCPs do not grant permissions, and with no allowing SCP in the path every other action is denied. This is why the AWS examples use a deny list strategy and keep a FullAWSAccess policy (or another policy that allows access) attached to the organization entities.`,[`orgs-scp-examples`,`orgs-scp-eval`],[`orgs-scp-examples|The SCP examples in this repository use a deny list strategy, which means that you also need a FullAWSAccess policy or other policy that allows access attached to your organization entities to allow actions.`,`orgs-scp-eval|SCP evaluation follows a deny-by-default model, meaning that any permissions not explicitly allowed in the SCPs are denied.`,`orgs-scp-eval|This is why when you enable SCPs, AWS Organizations attaches an AWS managed SCP policy named FullAWSAccess which allows all services and actions.`]),n(`An SCP can be attached to a root, an organizational unit (OU) or an account. It would have no effect on users or roles in the management account.`,[`orgs-policies-attach`,`orgs-scps`],[`orgs-policies-attach|To attach an authorization policy (SCP or RCP) to a root, OU, or account`,`orgs-scps|They have no effect on users or roles in the management account.`]),n(`Illustrative only: pick the actions to deny from your own requirements. AWS strongly recommends not attaching SCPs to the root of your organization without thoroughly testing the impact on accounts.`,`orgs-scps`,[`AWS strongly recommends that you don't attach SCPs to the root of your organization without thoroughly testing the impact that the policy has on accounts.`])]),t(`Bucket policy that lets one CloudFront distribution read an S3 bucket`,`bucket-policy`,`
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "AllowCloudFrontServicePrincipalReadOnly",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": { "Service": "cloudfront.amazonaws.com" },
      "Action": "s3:GetObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::example-site-bucket/*",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "AWS:SourceArn": "arn:aws:cloudfront::<ACCOUNT_ID>:distribution/<DISTRIBUTION_ID>"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
`,[n("This is a resource-based policy: it sits on the bucket, so it must name a `Principal`. Here the principal is an AWS service, the CloudFront service principal.",[`cf-oac`,`iam-el-principal`],[`Use an S3 bucket policy to allow the CloudFront service principal (cloudfront.amazonaws.com) to access the bucket.`,`iam-el-principal|You must use the Principal element in resource-based policies.`]),n("`Action` lists only `s3:GetObject`, so this statement allows only that action.",`iam-el-action`,[`The Action element describes the specific action or actions that will be allowed or denied.`]),n("The `Condition` element lets you specify conditions for when a policy is in effect. Here it matches on a distribution ARN, so the statement applies to requests from that distribution. The element is optional.",[`iam-el-condition`,`cf-oac`],[`The Condition element (or Condition block) lets you specify conditions for when a policy is in effect.`,`The Condition element is optional.`,`cf-oac|Use a Condition element in the policy to allow CloudFront to access the bucket only when the request is on behalf of the CloudFront distribution that contains the S3 origin.`]),n("Illustrative only: `<ACCOUNT_ID>` and `<DISTRIBUTION_ID>` are placeholders, and the bucket name is made up. The CloudFront documentation on restricting access to an S3 origin publishes its own example of a read-only bucket policy for a distribution with OAC enabled.",`cf-oac`,[`Example S3 bucket policy that allows read-only access for a CloudFront distribution with OAC enabled`],{newer:[`cloudfront-oac`]})]),t(`Trust policy for a role that another account may assume`,`iam-policy`,`
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": { "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::<TRUSTED_ACCOUNT_ID>:root" },
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole"
    }
  ]
}
`,[n("This is the role's trust policy. The `Principal` is another AWS account, which is how you name the account whose users may need access.",`iam-account-switch`,[`you specify the accounts by ID whose users need access in the Principal element of the role's trust policy.`]),n("The action is `sts:AssumeRole`: the trusted account's administrator must also grant their users or groups permission for that action before they can use it.",`iam-cross-account-role`,[`To do this, the administrator attaches a policy to the user or a group that grants permission for the sts:AssumeRole action.`]),n(`When the trusted entity is another AWS account, any IAM principal can be granted access to your resource, and that account can delegate the access. AWS offers IAM Access Analyzer to check which principals outside your zone of trust can assume your roles.`,[`iam-account-switch`,`iam-cross-account`],[`To learn whether principals in accounts outside of your zone of trust (trusted organization or account) have access to assume your roles, see What is IAM Access Analyzer?.`,`iam-cross-account|Whenever the trusted entity is another AWS account, any IAM principal can be granted access to your resource.`])])],confuse:[`iam-roles-vs-resource-policies`,`scp-boundary-identity`],azure:[{concept:`Azure management groups`,aws:`AWS Organizations`,mapping:n(`Management groups are how you organise Azure subscriptions; the Learn comparison pairs them with AWS Organizations, which groups AWS accounts and applies policy to them.`,[`learn-hub`,`orgs-intro`],[`learn-hub|Azure management groups help you organize your resources and subscriptions.`,`orgs-intro|group accounts to organize your workflows, apply policies for governance, and simplify billing`]),breaks:n(`In AWS, accounts are a hard boundary, and SCPs set a ceiling on permissions for the accounts underneath. SCPs do not grant permissions and have no effect on users or roles in the management account. Learn describes the comparison entry for Organizations and management groups as providing security policy and role management when you work with multiple accounts.`,[`sec-account-separation`,`orgs-scps`,`learn-hub`],[`sec-account-separation|In AWS, accounts are a hard boundary.`,`orgs-scps|SCPs do not grant permissions to the IAM users and IAM roles in your organization.`,`orgs-scps|They have no effect on users or roles in the management account.`,`learn-hub|These services provide security policy and role management when you work with multiple accounts.`])},{concept:`Azure Lighthouse and Azure landing zone`,aws:`AWS Control Tower`,mapping:n(`The Learn comparison pairs Control Tower with Azure Lighthouse and an Azure landing zone: all of them are about setting up and governing multi-account or multi-subscription environments.`,[`learn-hub`,`control-tower`],[`learn-hub|Set up and govern multiple-account or multiple-subscription environments.`,`control-tower|AWS Control Tower offers a straightforward way to set up and govern an AWS multi-account environment, following prescriptive best practices.`]),breaks:n(`Control Tower is a single AWS service that builds a landing zone and applies controls on top of Organizations, Service Catalog and IAM Identity Center, whereas the Learn comparison lists two separate Azure offerings against it.`,[`learn-hub`,`control-tower`],[`learn-hub|AWS Control Tower Azure LighthouseAzure landing zone`,`control-tower|AWS Control Tower orchestrates the capabilities of several other AWS services, including AWS Organizations, AWS Service Catalog, and AWS IAM Identity Center, to build a landing zone`],{allow:[`two`]})}]},l={building:`federation-bridge`,overview:[n(`Organisations that already have a place where their people are defined, such as a corporate directory or identity provider, can use {{term:federation}} to let those people sign in to AWS with that existing identity instead of getting a separate IAM user each. A design question here is which AWS service connects the directory to AWS, and whether it should.`,[`iam-saml`,`sso-what-is`],[`iam-saml|so users can log into the AWS Management Console or call AWS API operations without you having to create an IAM user for everyone in your organization.`,`sso-what-is|You can connect your existing identity provider and synchronize users and groups from your directory, or create and manage your users directly in IAM Identity Center.`]),n(`For workforce access, {{rename:iam-identity-center}} is the AWS service built for connecting your workforce users to AWS accounts and applications. You federate once, and with a SAML 2.0 identity provider you manage a single certificate.`,`sso-what-is`,[`AWS IAM Identity Center is the AWS solution for connecting your workforce users to AWS managed applications`,`With IAM Identity Center, you only federate once, and you have only one certificate to manage when using a SAML 2.0 identity provider.`])],bullets:[{id:`1.1-K2`,concepts:[n(`IAM supports SAML 2.0 federation, which enables federated single sign-on: users from a SAML identity provider get console or API access through an IAM role instead of an IAM user.`,`iam-saml`,[`This feature enables federated single sign-on (SSO), so users can log into the AWS Management Console or call AWS API operations`]),n(`IAM Identity Center can use its own user store, an external identity provider, or Active Directory as the identity source. With Active Directory it can connect a self-managed directory or a directory in AWS Managed Microsoft AD through AWS Directory Service.`,[`sso-what-is`,`sso-ad`],[`sso-what-is|You can connect your existing identity provider and synchronize users and groups from your directory, or create and manage your users directly in IAM Identity Center.`,`sso-ad|you can connect a self-managed directory in Active Directory (AD) or a directory in AWS Managed Microsoft AD by using AWS Directory Service`]),n(`For several AWS accounts, IAM Identity Center works with AWS Organizations: an organization instance is deployed in the management account and gives you a single point from which to manage user access across AWS, and permission sets centrally create permissions for common job functions that you provision across accounts and assign to users and groups.`,`sso-what-is`,[`An organization instance is deployed in the AWS Organizations management account and gives you a single point from which to manage user access across AWS.`,`You can also use IAM Identity Center permission sets to centrally create permissions for common job functions such as admin, provision them across AWS accounts, and assign them to users and groups.`]),n(`IAM roles are what federated users end up using: when you assume a role you get temporary security credentials for the role session.`,[`iam-roles`,`iam-saml`],[`Instead, when you assume a role, it provides you with temporary security credentials for your role session.`,`iam-saml|The client app calls the AWS STS AssumeRoleWithSAML API, passing the ARN of the SAML provider, the ARN of the role to assume, and the SAML assertion from IdP.`])],services:[`AWS IAM Identity Center`,`IAM`,`AWS Directory Service`],design:[n(`Workforce users who need access to several AWS accounts and applications: use IAM Identity Center with the corporate identity provider as the source. Single application with its own end users: a different tool (Amazon Cognito, see Warden's Lodge).`,[`sso-what-is`,`cognito-what-is`],[`AWS IAM Identity Center is the AWS solution for connecting your workforce users to AWS managed applications`,`cognito-what-is|With a user pool, your users can sign in to your web or mobile app through Amazon Cognito, or federate through a third-party IdP.`])]},{id:`1.1-S6`,concepts:[n(`{{term:aws-directory-service}} provides several ways to use Microsoft Active Directory (AD) with other AWS services. AWS Managed Microsoft AD is powered by an actual Windows Server Active Directory managed by AWS. AD Connector is a proxy that lets compatible AWS applications use your existing on-premises directory.`,`ds-what-is`,[`AWS Directory Service provides multiple ways to use Microsoft Active Directory (AD) with other AWS services.`,`Also known as AWS Managed Microsoft AD, AWS Directory Service for Microsoft Active Directory is powered by an actual Microsoft Windows Server Active Directory (AD), managed by AWS in the AWS Cloud.`,`AD Connector is a proxy service that provides an easy way to connect compatible AWS applications, such as Amazon WorkSpaces, Amazon Quick, and Amazon EC2 for Windows Server instances, to your existing on-premises Microsoft Active Directory.`]),n(`AD Connector also removes the need for directory synchronization or the cost and complexity of hosting a federation infrastructure.`,`ds-what-is`,[`AD Connector also eliminates the need of directory synchronization or the cost and complexity of hosting a federation infrastructure.`]),n(`AWS Managed Microsoft AD can instead store credentials in AWS, or connect to your existing AD infrastructure with a trust and use credentials from an Active Directory running on-premises or on EC2 Windows. If you join EC2 instances to it, users get the same Windows single sign-on experience as in your on-premises network.`,`ds-what-is`,[`All compatible applications work with user credentials that you store in AWS Managed Microsoft AD, or you can connect to your existing AD infrastructure with a trust and use credentials from an Active Directory running on-premises or on EC2 Windows.`,`If you join EC2 instances to your AWS Managed Microsoft AD, your users can access Windows workloads in the AWS Cloud with the same Windows single sign-on (SSO) experience as when they access workloads in your on-premises network.`])],services:[`AWS Directory Service`,`AWS IAM Identity Center`,`IAM`],design:[n(`Decide to federate when the people already exist in a directory: the alternative, an IAM user per person, adds credentials to manage. Choose AWS Directory Service when applications on AWS need Active Directory itself (for example domain-joined Windows workloads), and IAM Identity Center or SAML federation when the goal is signing people in to AWS.`,[`iam-users`,`ds-what-is`,`sso-what-is`,`iam-saml`],[`iam-users|An IAM user consists of a name and credentials.`,`sso-what-is|AWS IAM Identity Center is the AWS solution for connecting your workforce users to AWS managed applications`,`iam-saml|so users can log into the AWS Management Console or call AWS API operations without you having to create an IAM user for everyone in your organization.`,`iam-users|IAM best practices recommend that you require human users to use federation with an identity provider to access AWS using temporary credentials`,`ds-what-is|AWS Directory Service provides multiple directory choices for customers who want to use existing Microsoft AD or Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP)–aware applications in the cloud.`])]}],cues:[e(`single sign-on for employees across multiple AWS accounts`,`AWS IAM Identity Center`,n(`IAM Identity Center is the AWS solution for connecting workforce users to AWS applications and resources.`,`sso-what-is`,[`AWS IAM Identity Center is the AWS solution for connecting your workforce users to AWS managed applications`])),e(`without creating an IAM user for everyone`,`federation (SAML 2.0 or IAM Identity Center)`,n(`SAML federation lets users sign in without an IAM user for everyone in the organisation.`,`iam-saml`,[`without you having to create an IAM user for everyone in your organization`])),e(`existing on-premises Active Directory`,`AD Connector (proxy to the on-premises directory) or AWS Managed Microsoft AD (trust to it)`,n(`AD Connector proxies compatible AWS applications to an existing on-premises Microsoft Active Directory, whereas AWS Managed Microsoft AD can connect to existing AD infrastructure with a trust.`,`ds-what-is`,[`to your existing on-premises Microsoft Active Directory`,`you can connect to your existing AD infrastructure with a trust and use credentials from an Active Directory running on-premises or on EC2 Windows`])),e(`domain-joined Windows servers or LDAP-aware applications in AWS`,`AWS Directory Service`,n(`AWS Directory Service provides directory choices for customers using Microsoft AD or LDAP-aware applications in the cloud.`,`ds-what-is`,[`AWS Directory Service provides multiple directory choices for customers who want to use existing Microsoft AD or Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP)–aware applications in the cloud.`]))],examples:[],confuse:[],azure:[{concept:`Microsoft Entra ID`,aws:`AWS IAM Identity Center and IAM`,mapping:n(`Entra ID is the identity service you know. The Learn comparison pairs it with both IAM Identity Center and IAM because together they control who can reach AWS services and resources.`,[`learn-hub`,`sso-what-is`],[`learn-hub|Use these services to more securely control access to services and resources and improve data security and protection.`,`sso-what-is|AWS IAM Identity Center is the AWS solution for connecting your workforce users to AWS managed applications`]),breaks:n(`Learn lists one Azure service, Microsoft Entra ID, against two AWS ones, and describes it as creating and managing users and groups and using permissions to allow and deny access to resources. In AWS, IAM Identity Center connects workforce users to AWS and can take its users from your existing identity provider or from a store you create in Identity Center itself, while IAM holds the permissions.`,[`learn-hub`,`sso-what-is`,`iam-intro`],[`learn-hub|Create and manage users and groups, and use permissions to allow and deny access to resources.`,`sso-what-is|You can connect your existing identity provider and synchronize users and groups from your directory, or create and manage your users directly in IAM Identity Center.`,`iam-intro|With IAM, you can manage permissions that control which AWS resources users can access.`],{allow:[`two`]})},{concept:`Microsoft Entra Domain Services`,aws:`AWS Directory Service`,mapping:n(`Entra Domain Services provides managed domain services such as domain join, Group Policy, LDAP and Kerberos/NTLM authentication. AWS Directory Service offers managed Active Directory for the same kind of workload.`,[`learn-hub`,`ds-what-is`],[`learn-hub|Domain Services provides managed domain services, such as domain join, Group Policy, LDAP, and Kerberos/NTLM authentication, which are fully compatible with Windows Server Active Directory.`,`ds-what-is|AWS Directory Service provides multiple ways to use Microsoft Active Directory (AD) with other AWS services.`]),breaks:n(`AWS Directory Service is a family of options rather than one product: AWS Managed Microsoft AD, AD Connector (a proxy to your on-premises directory), and others.`,[`ds-what-is`,`learn-hub`],[`ds-what-is|AWS Directory Service includes several directory types to choose from.`,`learn-hub|AWS Directory Service Microsoft Entra Domain Services`])}]},u={building:`gatehouse`,overview:[n(`A {{term:vpc|VPC}} is your own logically isolated virtual network in AWS. Security inside it is built from four pieces: {{term:subnet|subnets}} that split the address space, {{term:route-table|route tables}} that decide where traffic goes, {{term:security-group|security groups}} on resources, and {{term:network-acl|network ACLs}} on subnets. A NAT gateway lets instances in a private subnet connect to services outside your VPC, while external services cannot initiate a connection to those instances.`,[`vpc-how-it-works`,`vpc-route-tables`,`vpc-security-groups`,`vpc-nacls`,`vpc-nat-gateway`],[`vpc-how-it-works|you can launch AWS resources in a logically isolated virtual network`,`vpc-route-tables|Each route table contains a set of rules, called routes, that determine where network traffic from your subnet or gateway is directed.`,`vpc-security-groups|A security group controls the traffic that is allowed to reach and leave the resources that it is associated with.`,`vpc-nacls|A network access control list (ACL) allows or denies specific inbound or outbound traffic at the subnet level.`,`vpc-nat-gateway|You can use a NAT gateway so that instances in a private subnet can connect to services outside your VPC but external services can't initiate a connection with those instances.`],{allow:[`four`]})],bullets:[{id:`1.2-K3`,concepts:[n(`Traffic is controlled by protocol, port range and source or destination. A security group rule has a protocol, a port range (for TCP and UDP) and a source (inbound) or destination (outbound); the most common protocols are TCP, UDP and ICMP.`,`vpc-sg-rules`,[`Protocol: The protocol to allow.`,`Port range: For TCP, UDP, or a custom protocol, the range of ports to allow.`,`Source or destination: The source (inbound rules) or destination (outbound rules) for the traffic to allow.`]),n(`Security groups are stateful and allow-only. Network ACLs are stateless, have inbound and outbound rules that are numbered and evaluated from the lowest number, and can deny. A VPC comes with a default security group and a default network ACL.`,[`vpc-security-groups`,`vpc-nacls`,`vpc-default-nacl`,`vpc-sg-rules`],[`vpc-security-groups|Security groups are stateful.`,`vpc-sg-rules|You can specify allow rules, but not deny rules.`,`vpc-security-groups|When you create a VPC, it comes with a default security group.`,`vpc-default-nacl|Your virtual private cloud (VPC) automatically comes with a default network ACL.`,`vpc-nacls|Network ACL rules A network ACL has inbound rules and outbound rules.`,`vpc-nacls|We evaluate the rules in order, starting with the lowest numbered rule, when deciding whether allow or deny traffic.`]),n(`The default network ACL and a new custom network ACL behave differently. The default one is configured to allow all traffic in and out of its subnets, whereas the initial rules of a custom network ACL block all inbound and outbound traffic until you add rules, and a custom ACL is not associated with a subnet until you associate it.`,[`vpc-default-nacl`,`vpc-create-nacl`],[`vpc-default-nacl|A default network ACL is configured to allow all traffic to flow in and out of the subnets with which it is associated.`,`vpc-create-nacl|The initial rules for a custom network ACL block all inbound and outbound traffic.`,`vpc-create-nacl|Your new custom network ACL is not associated with a subnet by default and must be explicitly associated with subnets.`]),n(`Because network ACLs are stateless, return traffic needs its own rule, and the reply goes to an ephemeral port chosen by the client. The range depends on the client: Elastic Load Balancing, a NAT gateway and Lambda functions use ports 1024-65535, and AWS says that opening ephemeral ports 1024-65535 covers the different types of clients.`,`vpc-custom-nacl`,[`For every rule that you add, there must be an inbound or outbound rule that allows response traffic.`,`The client that initiates the request chooses the ephemeral port range.`,`Requests originating from Elastic Load Balancing use ports 1024-65535.`,`A NAT gateway uses ports 1024-65535.`,`AWS Lambda functions use ports 1024-65535.`,`In practice, to cover the different types of clients that might initiate traffic to public-facing instances in your VPC, you can open ephemeral ports 1024-65535.`]),n(`Route tables send traffic by destination: each route has a destination (a CIDR block or prefix list) and a target such as an internet gateway, NAT gateway, VPC peering connection or VPN connection. The most specific matching route wins (the longest prefix match).`,[`vpc-route-tables`,`vpc-route-priority`],[`vpc-route-tables|Each route specifies a destination (CIDR block or prefix list) and a target (such as an internet gateway, NAT gateway, VPC peering connection, or VPN connection).`,`vpc-route-priority|In general, we direct traffic using the most specific route that matches the traffic.`])],services:[`Amazon VPC`],design:[n(`A typical secure pattern is to let web servers accept traffic only from the load balancer in front of them: the web servers' security group allows HTTP and HTTPS only from the load balancer. A rule that names a security group as its source or destination affects all instances associated with that group.`,`vpc-sg-rules`,[`Add rules to the security group for the web servers to allow HTTP and HTTPS traffic only from the load balancer.`,`When you specify a security group as the source or destination for a rule, the rule affects all instances that are associated with the security groups.`])]},{id:`1.2-S1`,concepts:[n(`An internet gateway is a horizontally scaled, redundant and highly available VPC component that allows communication between your VPC and the internet. It provides a route-table target for internet-routable traffic, and resources in public subnets reach the internet through it if they have a public IPv4 or IPv6 address.`,`vpc-igw`,[`An internet gateway is a horizontally scaled, redundant, and highly available VPC component that allows communication between your VPC and the internet.`,`An internet gateway provides a target in your VPC route tables for internet-routable traffic.`,`An internet gateway enables resources in your public subnets (such as EC2 instances) to connect to the internet if the resource has a public IPv4 address or an IPv6 address.`]),n(`A NAT gateway lets instances in a private subnet connect to services outside the VPC while external services cannot initiate a connection to them. A public NAT gateway lives in a public subnet and needs an Elastic IP address.`,`vpc-nat-gateway`,[`You can use a NAT gateway so that instances in a private subnet can connect to services outside your VPC but external services can't initiate a connection with those instances.`,`You create a public NAT gateway in a public subnet and must associate an Elastic IP address with the NAT gateway at creation.`]),n(`Each NAT gateway is created in a specific Availability Zone and implemented with redundancy in that zone. If resources in several zones share one zonal NAT gateway and its zone goes down, resources in the other zones lose internet access, so AWS suggests a NAT gateway in each zone with matching routing.`,`vpc-nat-basics`,[`Each NAT gateway is created in a specific Availability Zone and implemented with redundancy in that zone.`,`If you have resources in multiple Availability Zones and they share one NAT gateway, and if the NAT gateway's Availability Zone is down, resources in the other Availability Zones lose internet access.`,`To improve resiliency, create a NAT gateway in each Availability Zone, and configure your routing to ensure that resources use the NAT gateway in the same Availability Zone.`]),n(`AWS also offers a regional NAT gateway. It automatically expands across Availability Zones based on your workload presence, whereas a zonal NAT gateway operates in a single Availability Zone.`,`vpc-nat-regional`,[`A regional NAT gateway automatically expands across Availability Zones based on your workload presence.`,`Unlike standard NAT gateways (referred to as zonal NAT gateways), which operate in a single Availability Zone, regional NAT gateways follow your workloads to provide automatic high availability.`],{newer:[`regional-nat-gateway`]}),n(`For outbound-only access over IPv6, AWS offers an egress-only internet gateway: it allows outbound IPv6 communication from instances in your VPC to the internet and prevents the internet from initiating an IPv6 connection. A NAT gateway is the option AWS names for outbound-only communication over IPv4.`,`vpc-egress-only-igw`,[`An egress-only internet gateway is a horizontally scaled, redundant, and highly available VPC component that allows outbound communication over IPv6 from instances in your VPC to the internet, and prevents the internet from initiating an IPv6 connection with your instances.`,`To enable outbound-only internet communication over IPv4, use a NAT gateway instead.`])],services:[`Amazon VPC`],design:[n(`Putting these together: a public subnet has a direct route to an internet gateway and is where a public NAT gateway is created; a private subnet has no direct route to an internet gateway and routes outbound internet traffic to the NAT device.`,[`vpc-subnets`,`vpc-route-options`,`vpc-nat-gateway`],[`vpc-subnets|Public subnet – The subnet has a direct route to an internet gateway.`,`vpc-subnets|Private subnet – The subnet does not have a direct route to an internet gateway.`,`vpc-route-options|Then add a route for the private subnet's route table that routes IPv4 internet traffic (0.0.0.0/0) to the NAT device.`,`vpc-nat-gateway|You create a public NAT gateway in a public subnet and must associate an Elastic IP address with the NAT gateway at creation.`])]},{id:`1.2-S2`,concepts:[n(`A public subnet has a direct route to an internet gateway and its resources can access the public internet; a private subnet has no direct route to an internet gateway. The route table, not the address range, is what makes a subnet public or private.`,`vpc-subnets`,[`Public subnet – The subnet has a direct route to an internet gateway.`,`Resources in a public subnet can access the public internet.`,`Private subnet – The subnet does not have a direct route to an internet gateway.`]),n(`Each subnet must reside entirely within one Availability Zone and cannot span zones, so a multi-AZ design uses one subnet per tier per zone.`,`vpc-subnets`,[`Each subnet must reside entirely within one Availability Zone and cannot span zones.`]),n(`A subnet can be associated with only one network ACL at a time, while the network ACL rules apply to the subnet as a whole.`,`vpc-nacls`,[`However, a subnet can be associated with only one network ACL at a time.`])],services:[`Amazon VPC`],design:[n(`Study guidance: segment by exposure, keeping anything that needs no direct internet path in private subnets, which have no direct route to an internet gateway. AWS notes that security groups can meet your needs in most cases, with network ACLs as an additional layer of security.`,[`vpc-subnets`,`vpc-nacls`],[`vpc-subnets|Private subnet – The subnet does not have a direct route to an internet gateway.`,`vpc-subnets|In most cases, security groups can meet your needs.`,`vpc-subnets|However, you can use network ACLs if you want an additional layer of security.`,`vpc-nacls|Custom network ACLs add an additional layer of security to your VPC.`])]}],cues:[e(`allow only specific ports and protocols to an instance`,`a security group`,n(`A security group controls traffic that reaches and leaves the resources it is associated with, by protocol, port range and source or destination.`,[`vpc-sg-rules`,`vpc-security-groups`],[`Protocol: The protocol to allow.`,`Port range: For TCP, UDP, or a custom protocol, the range of ports to allow.`,`Source or destination: The source (inbound rules) or destination (outbound rules) for the traffic to allow.`,`vpc-security-groups|A security group controls the traffic that is allowed to reach and leave the resources that it is associated with.`])),e(`explicitly block a specific IP address or range`,`a network ACL deny rule (security groups cannot deny)`,n(`Security groups have allow rules only; a network ACL can allow or deny.`,[`vpc-sg-rules`,`vpc-nacls`],[`You can specify allow rules, but not deny rules.`,`vpc-nacls|Each rule can either allow or deny traffic.`])),e(`instances in a private subnet need to download updates but must not be reachable from the internet`,`a NAT gateway in a public subnet`,n(`A NAT gateway lets private-subnet instances connect out while external services cannot initiate a connection to them.`,`vpc-nat-gateway`,[`external services can't initiate a connection with those instances`])),e(`public subnet`,`route to an internet gateway`,n(`A public subnet has a direct route to an internet gateway.`,`vpc-subnets`,[`Public subnet – The subnet has a direct route to an internet gateway.`])),e(`NAT gateway outage in one AZ takes down internet access for other AZs`,`a zonal NAT gateway in each Availability Zone (or a regional NAT gateway)`,n(`AWS recommends creating a NAT gateway in each Availability Zone and routing to the one in the same zone; a regional NAT gateway follows your workloads to provide automatic high availability.`,[`vpc-nat-basics`,`vpc-nat-regional`],[`vpc-nat-basics|To improve resiliency, create a NAT gateway in each Availability Zone, and configure your routing to ensure that resources use the NAT gateway in the same Availability Zone.`,`vpc-nat-regional|regional NAT gateways follow your workloads to provide automatic high availability`],{newer:[`regional-nat-gateway`]})),e(`IPv6 instances need outbound-only internet access`,`an egress-only internet gateway`,n(`An egress-only internet gateway allows outbound communication over IPv6 and prevents the internet from initiating an IPv6 connection.`,`vpc-egress-only-igw`,[`allows outbound communication over IPv6 from instances in your VPC to the internet, and prevents the internet from initiating an IPv6 connection with your instances`])),e(`return traffic is blocked on a subnet with a network ACL`,`add a rule for the response traffic on the ephemeral ports`,n(`For every network ACL rule you add, there must be an inbound or outbound rule that allows response traffic.`,`vpc-custom-nacl`,[`For every rule that you add, there must be an inbound or outbound rule that allows response traffic.`]))],examples:[t(`Three-tier security group rules`,`rules`,`
sg-alb   (load balancer)
  inbound   TCP 443   source 0.0.0.0/0
  outbound  TCP 8080  destination sg-app

sg-app   (application servers)
  inbound   TCP 8080  source sg-alb
  outbound  TCP 5432  destination sg-db
  outbound  TCP 443   destination 0.0.0.0/0   (updates, through a NAT gateway)

sg-db    (database)
  inbound   TCP 5432  source sg-app
`,[n(`Each rule names a protocol, a port range, and a source (inbound) or destination (outbound). Here every rule allows TCP on one port.`,`vpc-sg-rules`,[`Protocol: The protocol to allow.`,`Port range: For TCP, UDP, or a custom protocol, the range of ports to allow.`]),n("`sg-app` accepts traffic only from `sg-alb`, and `sg-db` only from `sg-app`. Referencing a security group as the source makes the rule apply to every instance associated with that group.",`vpc-sg-rules`,[`When you specify a security group as the source or destination for a rule, the rule affects all instances that are associated with the security groups.`]),n(`Nothing in this set denies anything, because security groups can specify allow rules but not deny rules. Because security groups are stateful, replies to allowed traffic need no extra rule.`,[`vpc-sg-rules`,`vpc-security-groups`],[`vpc-sg-rules|You can specify allow rules, but not deny rules.`,`vpc-security-groups|Security groups are stateful.`]),n("The extra outbound rule on `sg-app` names a protocol, a port range and a destination (here, HTTPS to any address, for updates). A security group only allows the traffic, and a route table decides where it goes: a private subnet can reach the internet through a NAT gateway.",[`vpc-sg-rules`,`vpc-route-options`,`vpc-route-tables`],[`Source or destination: The source (inbound rules) or destination (outbound rules) for the traffic to allow.`,`vpc-sg-rules|You can specify allow rules, but not deny rules.`,`vpc-route-tables|Each route table contains a set of rules, called routes, that determine where network traffic from your subnet or gateway is directed.`,`vpc-route-options|To enable instances in a private subnet to connect to the internet, you can create a NAT gateway or launch a NAT instance in a public subnet.`]),n(`Illustrative only: the ports and group names are made up for this example. The AWS documentation explains the rule components in detail.`,`vpc-sg-rules`,[`Components of a security group rule The following are the components of inbound and outbound security group rules`])]),t(`Route tables for a public and a private subnet`,`routes`,`
Route table: public-subnets
  Destination      Target
  0.0.0.0/0        igw-<ID>          (internet gateway)

Route table: private-subnets
  Destination      Target
  0.0.0.0/0        nat-<ID>          (NAT gateway in a public subnet)
`,[n("Each route is a destination (a CIDR block) and a target. A route of `0.0.0.0/0` to an internet gateway is what gives a subnet its internet path, so subnets using this table are public.",[`vpc-route-options`,`vpc-route-tables`,`vpc-subnets`],[`vpc-route-options|To do this, create and attach an internet gateway to your VPC, and then add a route with a destination of 0.0.0.0/0 for IPv4 traffic or ::/0 for IPv6 traffic, and a target of the internet gateway ID`,`vpc-route-tables|Each route specifies a destination (CIDR block or prefix list) and a target`,`vpc-subnets|Public subnet – The subnet has a direct route to an internet gateway.`]),n(`For the private subnets, the default route points to a NAT device in a public subnet, so instances there can reach the internet but cannot receive unsolicited inbound connections from it.`,[`vpc-route-options`,`vpc-nat-gateway`],[`Then add a route for the private subnet's route table that routes IPv4 internet traffic (0.0.0.0/0) to the NAT device.`,`vpc-nat-gateway|Instances in private subnets can connect to the internet through a public NAT gateway, but the instances can't receive unsolicited inbound connections from the internet.`]),n("When more than one route matches, the most specific one wins (the longest prefix match), which is why a `10.10.2.15/32` route beats a `10.10.2.0/24` route.",`vpc-route-priority`,[`Longest prefix (for example, 10.10.2.15/32 has priority over 10.10.2.0/24)`]),n("Illustrative only: `<ID>` stands for a real gateway identifier. A real route table also holds a local route, which covers traffic destined for a target within the VPC and routes it within the VPC.",`vpc-route-priority`,[`Any traffic destined for a target within the VPC (10.0.0.0/16) is covered by the local route, and therefore is routed within the VPC.`])]),t(`A network ACL that blocks one address range`,`rules`,`
Inbound rules
  Rule #   Type        Protocol  Port   Source            Allow/Deny
  100      HTTPS       TCP       443    203.0.113.0/24    DENY
  200      HTTPS       TCP       443    0.0.0.0/0         ALLOW
  *        All traffic All       All    0.0.0.0/0         DENY
`,[n("Rules are evaluated starting with the lowest number, and once a rule matches no further rules are evaluated. The `DENY` at rule `100` is checked before the broader `ALLOW` at rule `200`, so that range is blocked. Rule `200` allows HTTPS from other addresses, and the `*` rule at the bottom denies all other inbound IPv4 traffic that no numbered rule has already handled.",[`vpc-nacls`,`vpc-custom-nacl`],[`vpc-nacls|We evaluate the rules in order, starting with the lowest numbered rule, when deciding whether allow or deny traffic.`,`vpc-nacls|If the traffic matches a rule, the rule is applied and we do not evaluate any additional rules.`,`vpc-custom-nacl|Denies all inbound IPv4 traffic not already handled by a preceding rule (not modifiable).`,`vpc-custom-nacl|so the * rule for IPv4 traffic ultimately denies the packet`]),n(`Rule numbers can range from 1 to 32766.`,`vpc-nacls`,[`Each rule has a number from 1 to 32766.`]),n(`Because network ACLs are stateless, this table only covers inbound traffic. Responses to allowed inbound traffic are not automatically allowed, so a real subnet also needs outbound rules that allow the response traffic, on the ephemeral ports the clients use.`,[`vpc-nacls`,`vpc-custom-nacl`],[`vpc-nacls|If, for example, you create a NACL rule to allow specific inbound traffic to a subnet, responses to that traffic are not automatically allowed.`,`vpc-custom-nacl|For every rule that you add, there must be an inbound or outbound rule that allows response traffic.`]),n("Illustrative only: `203.0.113.0/24` is a documentation range standing in for the addresses to block. Network ACLs apply to the subnet and a subnet has one network ACL at a time.",`vpc-nacls`,[`However, a subnet can be associated with only one network ACL at a time.`])])],confuse:[`sg-vs-nacl`],azure:[{concept:`Network security groups (NSGs)`,aws:`Security groups and network ACLs`,mapping:n(`Microsoft Learn pairs security groups with Azure network security groups: Azure NSGs are stateful and can be applied at the subnet or network-interface level.`,[`learn-networking`,`vpc-security-groups`],[`learn-networking|Azure uses stateful network security groups (NSGs), which can be applied at the subnet or NIC level`,`vpc-security-groups|Security groups are stateful.`]),breaks:n(`One Azure concept covers two AWS ones. An NSG can be applied at the subnet or NIC level, whereas AWS splits the roles: a security group is per resource and allow-only, and a network ACL is per subnet, stateless and can deny.`,[`learn-networking`,`vpc-nacls`,`vpc-sg-rules`],[`learn-networking|can be applied at the subnet or NIC level`,`vpc-nacls|NACLs are stateless, which means that information about previously sent or received traffic is not saved.`,`vpc-sg-rules|You can specify allow rules, but not deny rules.`],{allow:[`two`]})},{concept:`Azure Virtual Network and subnets`,aws:`Amazon VPC and subnets`,mapping:n(`Azure virtual networks and AWS VPCs are similar in that both provide isolated, logically defined network spaces.`,[`learn-networking`,`vpc-how-it-works`],[`learn-networking|Azure virtual networks and AWS virtual private clouds (VPCs) are similar in that they both provide isolated, logically defined network spaces within their respective cloud platforms.`,`vpc-how-it-works|you can launch AWS resources in a logically isolated virtual network`]),breaks:n(`Subnet placement differs. In AWS each subnet is bound to a single Availability Zone, so zonal redundancy needs one subnet per zone; in Azure a subnet is a regional construct that spans all availability zones in the region. This changes how you draw a multi-AZ design.`,[`learn-networking`,`vpc-subnets`],[`learn-networking|In AWS, each subnet is bound to a single Availability Zone, so achieving zonal redundancy requires creating one subnet per Availability Zone.`,`learn-networking|In Azure, a subnet is a regional construct that spans all availability zones in the region.`,`vpc-subnets|Each subnet must reside entirely within one Availability Zone and cannot span zones.`])},{concept:`User Defined Routes`,aws:`Route tables`,mapping:n(`Learn says that AWS route tables contain routes that direct traffic from a subnet or gateway subnet to the destination, and that the corresponding Azure feature is called user-defined routes. In AWS each route has a destination CIDR block or prefix list and a target such as an internet gateway or NAT gateway.`,[`learn-networking`,`vpc-route-tables`],[`learn-networking|AWS route tables contain routes that direct traffic from a subnet or gateway subnet to the destination. In Azure, the corresponding feature is called user-defined routes.`,`vpc-route-tables|Each route specifies a destination (CIDR block or prefix list) and a target (such as an internet gateway, NAT gateway, VPC peering connection, or VPN connection).`]),breaks:n(`Learn describes user-defined routes as custom or static routes that override the default Azure system routes. AWS says that in general it directs traffic using the most specific route that matches the traffic.`,[`vpc-route-priority`,`learn-networking`],[`learn-networking|These routes override the default Azure system routes.`,`vpc-route-priority|In general, we direct traffic using the most specific route that matches the traffic.`])}]},d={building:`identity-keep`,overview:[n(`Every secure design starts with answering "who can do what to which resource?". In AWS that is {{term:iam}}: you create identities (users, groups and roles), attach policies to say what they may do, and keep the {{term:root-user}} out of daily use. AWS's IAM best practices point the same way: grant only the permissions required, and use temporary credentials.`,[`iam-intro`,`iam-best-practices`],[`iam-intro|With IAM, you can manage permissions that control which AWS resources users can access.`,`iam-best-practices|Apply least-privilege permissions When you set permissions with IAM policies, grant only the permissions required to perform a task.`,`iam-best-practices|Require workloads to use temporary credentials with IAM roles to access AWS`])],beyondProject:[n(`If you have built a Lambda function, it runs with an execution role: an IAM role that grants the function permission to use other AWS services. SAA adds the design judgement around that role: scope it to the actions and resources the function needs, and know that Lambda assumes the role for you when the function is invoked.`,[`lambda-exec-role`,`iam-best-practices`],[`A Lambda Event Function's execution role is an AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) role that grants the function permission to access AWS services and resources.`,`Lambda automatically assumes your execution role when you invoke your function.`,`iam-best-practices|You do this by defining the actions that can be taken on specific resources under specific conditions, also known as least-privilege permissions.`]),n(`When a design could use either a broad managed policy or a narrowly scoped custom policy for the same job, AWS's least-privilege guidance favours the narrow one: define the actions that can be taken on specific resources under specific conditions.`,`iam-best-practices`,[`You do this by defining the actions that can be taken on specific resources under specific conditions, also known as least-privilege permissions.`])],bullets:[{id:`1.1-K4`,concepts:[n(`{{term:least-privilege}} means granting only the permissions required to perform a task. AWS describes it as defining the actions that can be taken on specific resources under specific conditions, and says you can start from what the use case needs and work to reduce permissions as it matures.`,`iam-best-practices`,[`Apply least-privilege permissions When you set permissions with IAM policies, grant only the permissions required to perform a task.`,`As your use case matures, you can work to reduce the permissions that you grant to work toward least privilege.`]),n(`AWS's IAM best practices push temporary credentials: require human users to use federation with an identity provider, and require workloads to use IAM roles. Both avoid long-lived access keys.`,`iam-best-practices`,[`Require your human users to use temporary credentials when accessing AWS.`,`Require workloads to use temporary credentials with IAM roles to access AWS`]),n(`Other best practices in the same list include requiring {{term:mfa}} and updating access keys when long-term credentials are unavoidable.`,`iam-best-practices`,[`Require multi-factor authentication (MFA)`,`Update access keys when needed for use cases that require long-term credentials`])],services:[`IAM`],design:[n(`In a design, AWS's guidance is to give workloads IAM roles for temporary credentials, to give people federated sign-in through an identity provider, and to require MFA wherever there is an IAM user or root user.`,[`iam-best-practices`,`iam-roles`],[`iam-best-practices|Require workloads to use temporary credentials with IAM roles to access AWS`,`iam-best-practices|Require human users to use federation with an identity provider to access AWS using temporary credentials`,`iam-best-practices|for scenarios in which you need an IAM user or root user in your account, require MFA for additional security.`,`iam-roles|Also, a role does not have standard long-term credentials such as a password or access keys associated with it.`])]},{id:`1.1-S1`,concepts:[n(`Root user: it has complete access to the account, so use it only for tasks that need root-level permissions. AWS lists those tasks on a dedicated page, and states that MFA is enforced for the root user of all account types.`,[`iam-root-user`,`iam-mfa`],[`iam-root-user|Use the root user only to perform the tasks that require root-level permissions.`,`iam-mfa|MFA is enforced for all account types for their root user.`]),n(`Root user access keys: AWS strongly recommends not creating access keys for the root user, because the root user has full access to all AWS services and resources in the account. It suggests creating an administrative user for everyday tasks instead.`,`iam-root-best-practices`,[`We strongly recommend that you do not create access keys for your root user because the root user has full access to all AWS services and resources in the account, including billing information.`,`Instead of accessing the root user, create an administrative user for everyday tasks.`]),n(`MFA: AWS recommends phishing-resistant MFA such as passkeys and security keys whenever possible. Those FIDO-based authenticators use public key cryptography and resist phishing, man-in-the-middle and replay attacks.`,`iam-mfa`,[`We recommend that you use phishing-resistant MFA such as passkeys and security keys whenever possible.`,`These FIDO-based authenticators use public key cryptography and are resistant to phishing, man-in-the-middle, and replay attacks`],{newer:[`iam-passkey-mfa`]}),n(`IAM users: an IAM user is an entity you create in your AWS account, and AWS's best practices say human users should use federation with an identity provider to get temporary credentials instead.`,`iam-users`,[`An IAM user is an entity that you create in your AWS account.`,`IAM best practices recommend that you require human users to use federation with an identity provider to access AWS using temporary credentials`])],services:[`IAM`],design:[n(`A design that follows AWS guidance enables MFA on the root user and on any IAM user, creates no access keys for the root user, and gives people an administrative user or federated sign-in rather than the root user for everyday tasks.`,[`iam-root-user`,`iam-best-practices`,`iam-root-best-practices`],[`iam-root-user|Use the root user only to perform the tasks that require root-level permissions.`,`iam-best-practices|for scenarios in which you need an IAM user or root user in your account, require MFA for additional security.`,`iam-root-best-practices|We strongly recommend that you do not create access keys for your root user because the root user has full access to all AWS services and resources in the account, including billing information.`,`iam-root-best-practices|Instead of accessing the root user, create an administrative user for everyday tasks.`])]},{id:`1.1-S2`,concepts:[n(`Building blocks: an {{term:iam-user}} is an entity you create in your account; an {{term:iam-group}} is a collection of IAM users; an {{term:iam-role}} is an identity with specific permissions that has no long-term password or access keys and gives temporary credentials when assumed.`,[`iam-users`,`iam-groups`,`iam-roles`],[`iam-users|An IAM user is an entity that you create in your AWS account.`,`iam-groups|An IAM user group is a collection of IAM users.`,`iam-roles|Instead, when you assume a role, it provides you with temporary security credentials for your role session.`]),n(`A {{term:policy}} is an object that, when associated with an identity or resource, defines their permissions. AWS supports nine policy types, including identity-based policies, resource-based policies, permissions boundaries, SCPs and session policies; identity-based policies grant permissions to an identity.`,`iam-policies`,[`A policy is an object in AWS that, when associated with an identity or resource, defines their permissions.`,`AWS supports nine types of policies: identity-based policies, resource-based policies, VPC endpoint policies, permissions boundaries, AWS Organizations service control policies (SCPs)`,`Identity-based policies grant permissions to an identity.`]),n(`Identity-based policies can be managed (AWS managed or customer managed, reusable) or inline (tied one-to-one to a single identity).`,`iam-managed-inline`,[`AWS managed policies Standalone policy created and administered by AWS.`,`Policy created for a single IAM identity (user, group, or role) that maintains a strict one-to-one relationship between a policy and an identity.`]),n(`When a request arrives, AWS evaluates all the policy types that apply, and an explicit deny in any of the policies overrides an allow.`,[`iam-eval-logic`,`iam-policy-eval-basics`],[`iam-eval-logic|AWS evaluates all of the policy types and the order of the policies affects how they are evaluated.`,`iam-policy-eval-basics|An explicit deny in any of the policies overrides the allow.`])],services:[`IAM`],design:[n(`One way to keep permissions manageable is to attach them to a group rather than to each user, because a user group lets you specify permissions for multiple users. A managed policy is standalone, whereas an inline policy keeps a strict one-to-one relationship with a single identity, so it fits when that tie is intended.`,[`iam-groups`,`iam-managed-inline`],[`iam-groups|User groups let you specify permissions for multiple users, which can make it easier to manage the permissions for those users.`,`iam-managed-inline|An AWS managed policy is a standalone policy that is created and administered by AWS.`,`iam-managed-inline|Policy created for a single IAM identity (user, group, or role) that maintains a strict one-to-one relationship between a policy and an identity.`]),n(`A group cannot be a principal in a policy, because groups relate to permissions, not authentication. If a question wants to grant a resource policy to "the team", it needs the users or a role, not the group.`,`iam-el-principal`,[`You cannot identify a user group as a principal in a policy (such as a resource-based policy) because groups relate to permissions, not authentication, and principals are authenticated IAM entities.`])]}],cues:[e(`grant only the permissions required`,`least-privilege policy scoped to specific actions and resources`,n(`IAM best practices define least privilege as granting only the permissions required to perform a task.`,`iam-best-practices`,[`grant only the permissions required to perform a task`])),e(`application running on EC2 / Lambda needs access to another AWS service`,`an IAM role (temporary credentials), not stored access keys`,n(`AWS says to require workloads to use temporary credentials with IAM roles.`,`iam-best-practices`,[`Require workloads to use temporary credentials with IAM roles to access AWS`])),e(`human users, a corporate identity provider`,`federation / IAM Identity Center rather than individual IAM users`,n(`AWS recommends requiring human users to use federation with an identity provider to access AWS with temporary credentials.`,`iam-users`,[`IAM best practices recommend that you require human users to use federation with an identity provider to access AWS using temporary credentials`])),e(`explicitly deny / even if another policy allows it`,`an explicit deny overrides an allow`,n(`An explicit deny in any of the policies overrides the allow.`,`iam-policy-eval-basics`,[`An explicit deny in any of the policies overrides the allow.`])),e(`secure the account root user`,`enable MFA, create no access keys for it and avoid daily use`,n(`Use the root user only for tasks that require root-level permissions, and MFA is enforced for the root user of all account types.`,[`iam-root-user`,`iam-mfa`],[`iam-root-user|Use the root user only to perform the tasks that require root-level permissions.`,`iam-mfa|MFA is enforced for all account types for their root user.`]))],examples:[t(`Least-privilege identity policy`,`iam-policy`,`
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "ReadReportsOnly",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::example-reports-bucket/reports/*"
    }
  ]
}
`,[n("`Version` should always be set to `2012-10-17`, the current version of the policy language.",`iam-el-version`,[`This is the current version of the policy language, and you should always include a Version element and set it to 2012-10-17.`]),n("`Sid` is an optional identifier for the statement; it is a label, and it does not change what the statement does.",`iam-el-sid`,[`You can provide a Sid (statement ID) as an optional identifier for the policy statement.`]),n("`Effect` is required and says whether the statement results in an allow or an explicit deny. This statement allows.",`iam-el-effect`,[`The Effect element is required and specifies whether the statement results in an allow or an explicit deny.`]),n("`Action` names the specific action allowed. Only `s3:GetObject` is listed, so this statement allows only that action.",`iam-el-action`,[`The Action element describes the specific action or actions that will be allowed or denied.`]),n("`Resource` identifies what the action applies to, by Amazon Resource Name (ARN). Naming one bucket prefix is an example of defining the actions that can be taken on specific resources, which AWS calls least-privilege permissions.",[`iam-el-resource`,`iam-best-practices`],[`You specify a resource using an Amazon Resource Name (ARN).`,`Although the ARN format varies you always use an ARN to identify a resource.`,`iam-best-practices|You do this by defining the actions that can be taken on specific resources under specific conditions, also known as least-privilege permissions.`]),n("Illustrative only: the bucket name is a placeholder. There is no `Principal` because this is an identity-based policy, which is attached to a user, group or role and cannot use the Principal element.",`iam-el-principal`,[`You cannot use the Principal element in an identity-based policy.`])]),t(`Trust policy for a Lambda execution role`,`iam-policy`,`
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": { "Service": "lambda.amazonaws.com" },
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole"
    }
  ]
}
`,[n("This is a role trust policy: the `Principal` says who is allowed to assume the role. Here the trusted service is Lambda.",`iam-el-principal`,[`In IAM roles, use the Principal element in the role trust policy to specify who can assume the role.`]),n("For Lambda to assume the execution role, the role's trust policy must specify the Lambda service principal, `lambda.amazonaws.com`, as a trusted service.",`lambda-exec-role`,[`the role's trust policy must specify the Lambda service principal (lambda.amazonaws.com) as a trusted service`]),n(`A separate permissions policy (like the first example) says what the role can do once assumed. The trust policy and the permissions policy do different jobs.`,`iam-roles`,[`An IAM role is similar to an IAM user, in that it is an AWS identity with permission policies that determine what the identity can and cannot do in AWS.`])])],confuse:[],azure:[{concept:`Azure role-based access control (Azure RBAC)`,aws:`AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)`,mapping:n(`Azure RBAC is how you already decide who can do what to Azure resources. The Learn comparison pairs it with IAM, and describes the IAM and Entra ID entry as creating and managing users and groups and using permissions to allow and deny access to resources.`,[`learn-hub`,`iam-intro`],[`learn-hub|Azure RBAC helps you manage who can access Azure resources, which resources they can access, and what they can do with those resources.`,`learn-hub|Create and manage users and groups, and use permissions to allow and deny access to resources.`,`iam-intro|With IAM, you can manage permissions that control which AWS resources users can access.`]),breaks:n(`Take care with the word "role". Azure RBAC is described by Learn as managing who can access which resources and what they can do with them. An AWS IAM role is an identity that has specific permissions and is intended to be assumable by anyone who needs it, giving temporary security credentials for the role session; the permissions themselves are defined in policies associated with identities or resources.`,[`learn-hub`,`iam-roles`,`iam-policies`],[`learn-hub|Azure RBAC helps you manage who can access Azure resources`,`iam-roles|An IAM role is an IAM identity that you can create in your account that has specific permissions.`,`iam-roles|However, instead of being uniquely associated with one person, a role is intended to be assumable by anyone who needs it.`,`iam-roles|Instead, when you assume a role, it provides you with temporary security credentials for your role session.`,`iam-policies|A policy is an object in AWS that, when associated with an identity or resource, defines their permissions.`])},{concept:`Microsoft Entra ID (multi-factor authentication)`,aws:`MFA for IAM`,mapping:n(`The Learn comparison pairs Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) for IAM with Microsoft Entra ID, and describes the Entra ID side as helping safeguard access to data and applications while providing a straightforward sign-in process. AWS recommends configuring MFA to help protect your AWS resources.`,[`learn-hub`,`iam-mfa`],[`learn-hub|Help safeguard access to data and applications while providing a straightforward sign-in process to users`,`iam-mfa|we recommend that you configure multi-factor authentication (MFA) to help protect your AWS resources`]),breaks:n(`Learn lists the whole Microsoft Entra ID service against MFA for IAM, whereas the AWS feature is narrower: you can enable MFA for the AWS account root user and for IAM users, and AWS states that MFA is enforced for the root user of all account types.`,[`learn-hub`,`iam-mfa`],[`learn-hub|Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) for IAM Microsoft Entra ID`,`iam-mfa|You can enable MFA for the AWS account root user of all AWS accounts, including standalone accounts, management accounts, and member accounts, as well as for your IAM users.`,`iam-mfa|MFA is enforced for all account types for their root user.`])}]},f={building:`key-vault`,overview:[n(`Encryption protects data only as well as the keys are controlled. In AWS the managed answer is {{term:aws-kms|AWS KMS}}: it creates and controls the keys used to encrypt and sign your data, and many AWS services use KMS keys to encrypt the data they store on your behalf. Who can use a key is set by its {{term:key-policy|key policy}}. For full control of keys and algorithms on single-tenant HSMs there is AWS CloudHSM.`,[`kms-overview`,`kms-key-policies`,`cloudhsm`,`kms-concepts`],[`kms-overview|AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) is an AWS managed service that makes it easy for you to create and control the keys used to encrypt and sign your data.`,`kms-concepts|Customer managed keys can also be used in conjunction with AWS services that use KMS keys to encrypt the data the service stores on your behalf.`,`kms-key-policies|Key policies are the primary way to control access to KMS keys.`,`cloudhsm|A hardware security module (HSM) is a computing device that processes cryptographic operations and provides secure storage for cryptographic keys.`,`cloudhsm|Full control of your keys, algorithms, and application development AWS CloudHSM gives you full control of the algorithms and keys you use.`,`cloudhsm|We offer HSMs that are standards-compliant, single-tenant`])],bullets:[{id:`1.3-K4`,concepts:[n(`KMS keys you create and manage are customer managed keys, which AWS recommends for full control over their lifecycle and usage; there is a monthly cost for each customer managed key in your account. Keys that AWS creates for you in your account are AWS managed keys.`,`kms-concepts`,[`The KMS keys that you create and manage for use in your own cryptographic applications are of a type known as customer managed keys.`,`Customer managed keys are recommended for customers who want full control over the lifecycle and usage of their keys.`,`There is a monthly cost to have a customer managed key in your account.`]),n(`KMS keys never leave AWS KMS unencrypted, and the keys you create are protected by FIPS 140-3 Security Level 3 validated hardware security modules. If you need more, AWS KMS custom key stores can be backed by a dedicated CloudHSM cluster that you own, or by an external key manager.`,[`kms-overview`,`kms-key-stores`],[`kms-overview|They never leave AWS KMS unencrypted.`,`kms-overview|The AWS KMS keys that you create in AWS KMS are protected by FIPS 140-3 Security Level 3 validated hardware security modules (HSM).`,`kms-key-stores|There are two types: AWS CloudHSM key stores, backed by a dedicated, customer-owned AWS CloudHSM cluster, and external key stores, backed by an HSM or key management system outside the AWS Cloud.`],{allow:[`two`]}),n(`AWS CloudHSM gives you full control of the algorithms and keys, with single-tenant, standards-compliant HSMs. Use it when a requirement calls for single-tenant HSMs or full control of the algorithms and keys; compare it with KMS in the Don't confuse section.`,`cloudhsm`,[`Full control of your keys, algorithms, and application development AWS CloudHSM gives you full control of the algorithms and keys you use.`,`We offer HSMs that are standards-compliant, single-tenant`])],services:[`AWS KMS`,`AWS CloudHSM`],design:[n(`AWS KMS is the managed service whose keys other AWS services use to encrypt the data they store, and its key policies determine who may use a key. AWS CloudHSM is the option when a requirement calls for single-tenant HSMs or full control of the keys and algorithms.`,[`kms-key-policies`,`cloudhsm`,`kms-concepts`],[`kms-key-policies|The statements in the key policy determine who has permission to use the KMS key and how they can use it.`,`kms-concepts|Customer managed keys can also be used in conjunction with AWS services that use KMS keys to encrypt the data the service stores on your behalf.`,`cloudhsm|Full control of your keys, algorithms, and application development AWS CloudHSM gives you full control of the algorithms and keys you use.`])]},{id:`1.3-S2`,concepts:[n(`S3 encrypts every new object at rest by default using server-side encryption with Amazon S3 managed keys (SSE-S3), with no additional fees. To choose a different model you can specify SSE-KMS or DSSE-KMS on the request, or change the bucket's default encryption. SSE-C is also available but is disabled by default for new general purpose buckets (see below).`,[`s3-sse-s3-specify`,`s3-sse-s3`,`s3-sse-c`],[`s3-sse-s3-specify|Server-side encryption with Amazon S3 managed keys (SSE-S3) is the default encryption configuration for every bucket in Amazon S3.`,`s3-sse-s3-specify|you can use server-side encryption with AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) keys (SSE-KMS), dual-layer server-side encryption with AWS KMS keys (DSSE-KMS), or server-side encryption with customer-provided keys (SSE-C).`,`s3-sse-s3|There are no additional fees for using server-side encryption with Amazon S3 managed keys (SSE-S3).`,`s3-sse-c|Starting April 2026, SSE-C is disabled by default for all new general purpose buckets and existing buckets in accounts with no SSE-C encrypted objects.`],{newer:[`s3-default-sse-s3`,`s3-ssec-blocked-default`,`s3-dsse-kms`]}),n(`With SSE-KMS you configure buckets to use AWS KMS keys, and access to the KMS key is controlled by its key policy. With SSE-C you provide the encryption key as part of your request and S3 does the encrypting and decrypting. Compare SSE-S3, SSE-KMS and SSE-C below.`,[`s3-sse-kms`,`kms-key-policies`,`s3-sse-c`],[`s3-sse-kms|However, you can choose to configure buckets to use server-side encryption with AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) keys (SSE-KMS) instead.`,`kms-key-policies|Key policies are the primary way to control access to KMS keys.`,`s3-sse-c|With the encryption key that you provide as part of your request, Amazon S3 manages data encryption as it writes to disks and data decryption when you access your objects.`])],services:[`AWS KMS`,`Amazon S3`],design:[n(`Other AWS services follow the same idea: they use KMS keys to encrypt the data they store on your behalf. Customer managed keys can be used in conjunction with those services.`,`kms-concepts`,[`Customer managed keys can also be used in conjunction with AWS services that use KMS keys to encrypt the data the service stores on your behalf.`])]},{id:`1.3-S4`,concepts:[n(`A {{term:key-policy|key policy}} is a resource policy for a KMS key, every KMS key must have exactly one, and its statements determine who can use the key and how.`,`kms-key-policies`,[`A key policy is a resource policy for an AWS KMS key.`,`Every KMS key must have exactly one key policy.`,`The statements in the key policy determine who has permission to use the KMS key and how they can use it.`]),n(`The default key policy depends on how the key is created. Through the API it has one statement, which gives the AWS account permission to use IAM policies for all KMS operations on the key. The console adds key administrator and key user statements to that default.`,`kms-default-key-policy`,[`The default key policy that AWS KMS uses differs depending on whether you create the key in the AWS KMS console or you use the AWS KMS API.`,`This default key policy has one policy statement that gives the AWS account that owns the KMS key permission to use IAM policies to allow access to all AWS KMS operations on the KMS key.`,`The console then adds a key administrators statement, a key users statement, and (for most key types) a statement that allows principals to use the KMS key with other AWS services.`])],services:[`AWS KMS`,`IAM`],design:[n(`The default key policy that the console creates separates key administrators, who administer the key, from key users, who use it, and a design can follow that split by naming different roles for each. Because key policies are the primary control, an IAM policy alone is not enough unless the key policy enables IAM policies.`,[`kms-key-policies`,`kms-default-key-policy`],[`kms-key-policies|Key policies are the primary way to control access to KMS keys.`,`kms-default-key-policy|The console then adds a key administrators statement, a key users statement, and (for most key types) a statement that allows principals to use the KMS key with other AWS services.`,`kms-default-key-policy|gives the AWS account that owns the KMS key permission to use IAM policies to allow access to all AWS KMS operations on the KMS key`])]}],cues:[e(`manage and control the encryption keys / audit key usage`,`AWS KMS (customer managed key)`,n(`Customer managed keys are recommended when you want full control over key lifecycle and usage, and you can audit their usage in AWS CloudTrail logs.`,`kms-concepts`,[`Customer managed keys are recommended for customers who want full control over the lifecycle and usage of their keys.`,`You can use your customer managed key in cryptographic operations and audit usage in AWS CloudTrail logs.`])),e(`single-tenant hardware security module / full control of keys and algorithms`,`AWS CloudHSM`,n(`CloudHSM gives you full control of the algorithms and keys, with single-tenant HSMs.`,`cloudhsm`,[`We offer HSMs that are standards-compliant, single-tenant`])),e(`S3 objects must be encrypted at rest, no other requirement`,`already satisfied: SSE-S3 is the default`,n(`All new object uploads to S3 are encrypted by default with SSE-S3.`,`s3-sse-s3`,[`All new object uploads to Amazon S3 buckets are encrypted by default with server-side encryption with Amazon S3 managed keys (SSE-S3).`],{newer:[`s3-default-sse-s3`]})),e(`who can use the key / separate administrators from users`,`the KMS key policy`,n(`Key policies are the primary way to control access to KMS keys.`,`kms-key-policies`,[`Key policies are the primary way to control access to KMS keys.`])),e(`provide our own key with each request to S3`,`SSE-C`,n(`With SSE-C, S3 uses the key you provide with your request.`,`s3-sse-c`,[`With the encryption key that you provide as part of your request, Amazon S3 manages data encryption as it writes to disks and data decryption when you access your objects.`]))],examples:[t(`Key policy with an account statement, an administrator and a user`,`key-policy`,`
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "EnableIAMPolicies",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": { "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::<ACCOUNT_ID>:root" },
      "Action": "kms:*",
      "Resource": "*"
    },
    {
      "Sid": "KeyAdministrators",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": { "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::<ACCOUNT_ID>:role/KeyAdminRole" },
      "Action": ["kms:Create*", "kms:Describe*", "kms:Enable*", "kms:Put*", "kms:ScheduleKeyDeletion"],
      "Resource": "*"
    },
    {
      "Sid": "KeyUsers",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": { "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::<ACCOUNT_ID>:role/AppRole" },
      "Action": ["kms:Encrypt", "kms:Decrypt", "kms:GenerateDataKey*"],
      "Resource": "*"
    }
  ]
}
`,[n(`A key policy is a resource policy for the KMS key itself.`,`kms-key-policies`,[`A key policy is a resource policy for an AWS KMS key.`]),n(`The first statement enables IAM policies: it lets the account use IAM policies to allow access to KMS operations on the key. That also means IAM policies in the account can grant use of the key, in addition to the principals the later statements name, so the administrator and user statements are not the only way in.`,`kms-default-key-policy`,[`This default key policy has one policy statement that gives the AWS account that owns the KMS key permission to use IAM policies to allow access to all AWS KMS operations on the KMS key.`]),n(`The default key policy that the console creates distinguishes key administrators, who administer the key, from key users, who use it for cryptographic operations. The second and third statements follow that shape; the action lists are examples, not a complete or recommended set.`,`kms-default-key-policy`,[`The console then adds a key administrators statement, a key users statement, and (for most key types) a statement that allows principals to use the KMS key with other AWS services.`]),n("Illustrative only: `<ACCOUNT_ID>` and the role names are placeholders. The statements in a real key policy decide who can use the key and how, so review each one before relying on it.",`kms-key-policies`,[`The statements in the key policy determine who has permission to use the KMS key and how they can use it.`])])],confuse:[`kms-vs-cloudhsm`,`sse-s3-kms-c`],azure:[{concept:`Azure Key Vault and Managed HSM`,aws:`AWS KMS and AWS CloudHSM`,mapping:n(`The Learn comparison pairs Azure Key Vault and Key Vault Managed HSM with AWS KMS and CloudHSM. Learn says Key Vault provides a shared HSM or a dedicated HSM, and that on AWS KMS uses a shared HSM while CloudHSM is a dedicated HSM.`,[`learn-hub`,`kms-overview`],[`learn-hub|Key Vault provides a shared HSM or a dedicated HSM. On AWS, KMS uses a shared HSM. CloudHSM is a dedicated HSM.`,`kms-overview|AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) is an AWS managed service that makes it easy for you to create and control the keys used to encrypt and sign your data.`]),breaks:n(`Learn lists AWS KMS and CloudHSM together against Azure Key Vault, but in AWS they are two separate services. KMS is a managed service whose access is controlled by key policies; CloudHSM offers standards-compliant, single-tenant HSMs. Learn also lists Key Vault certificates against a third AWS service, AWS Certificate Manager.`,[`cloudhsm`,`kms-key-policies`,`learn-hub`],[`cloudhsm|We offer HSMs that are standards-compliant, single-tenant`,`kms-key-policies|Key policies are the primary way to control access to KMS keys.`,`learn-hub|AWS Key Management Service (KMS), AWS CloudHSM Azure Key Vault Azure Key Vault Managed HSM`,`learn-hub|Certificate Manager Key Vault certificates Microsoft Cloud PKI`],{allow:[`two`,`third`]})}]},p={building:`wardens-lodge`,overview:[n(`Applications need two kinds of protection that are easy to confuse: keeping their own secrets (database passwords, API keys) out of source code, and letting their users sign in. AWS Secrets Manager addresses the first (Systems Manager Parameter Store is a centralized configuration data store, and AWS recommends Secrets Manager for secrets). Amazon Cognito authenticates and authorizes users, and IAM Identity Center is the AWS solution for connecting your workforce users to AWS managed applications.`,[`secrets-intro`,`cognito-what-is`,`ssm-parameter-store`,`sso-what-is`],[`secrets-intro|Secrets Manager helps you improve your security posture, because you no longer need hard-coded credentials in application source code.`,`cognito-what-is|Users can sign in directly, through social providers like Google and Apple, or through enterprise SAML and OIDC providers.`,`cognito-what-is|With Amazon Cognito, you can authenticate and authorize users from the built-in user directory, from your enterprise directory, and from consumer identity providers like Google and Facebook.`,`ssm-parameter-store|Parameter Store is a centralized configuration data store for named values called parameters.`,`ssm-parameter-store|For secrets such as database credentials, API keys, or tokens, we recommend AWS Secrets Manager, which provides purpose built security controls including automatic rotation and cross-region replication.`,`sso-what-is|AWS IAM Identity Center is the AWS solution for connecting your workforce users to AWS managed applications`],{allow:[`two`]})],bullets:[{id:`1.2-K1`,concepts:[n(`AWS Secrets Manager helps you manage, retrieve and rotate database credentials, application credentials, OAuth tokens, API keys and other secrets throughout their lifecycles. The application makes a runtime call to retrieve the secret instead of holding it in its code.`,`secrets-intro`,[`AWS Secrets Manager helps you manage, retrieve, and rotate database credentials, application credentials, OAuth tokens, API keys, and other secrets throughout their lifecycles.`,`You replace hard-coded credentials with a runtime call to the Secrets Manager service to retrieve credentials dynamically when you need them.`]),n(`Rotation is the process of periodically updating a secret: when you rotate it, you update the credentials in both the secret and the database or service. Secrets Manager can rotate secrets automatically, in one of two ways: managed rotation, where the service configures and manages rotation for you without a Lambda function, or rotation by a Lambda function for other types of secrets.`,`secrets-rotation`,[`In Secrets Manager, you can set up automatic rotation for your secrets.`,`Rotation is the process of periodically updating a secret.`,`When you rotate a secret, you update the credentials in both the secret and the database or service.`,`Managed rotation doesn't use a Lambda function.`,`There are two forms of rotation: Managed rotation – For most managed secrets, you use managed rotation, where the service configures and manages rotation for you.`,`Rotation by Lambda function – For other types of secrets, Secrets Manager rotation uses a Lambda function to update the secret and the database or service.`],{allow:[`two`]}),n(`You can use the AWS managed key aws/secretsmanager that Secrets Manager creates to encrypt your secrets, or create your own KMS keys. The AWS managed key is free, while your own KMS keys are charged at the KMS rate.`,`secrets-intro`,[`You can use the AWS managed key aws/secretsmanager that Secrets Manager creates to encrypt your secrets for free.`,`If you create your own KMS keys to encrypt your secrets, AWS charges you at the current AWS KMS rate.`]),n(`Systems Manager Parameter Store is a centralized store for configuration data. It supports String, StringList and SecureString parameters; for secrets such as database credentials, API keys or tokens, AWS recommends Secrets Manager.`,`ssm-parameter-store`,[`Parameter Store is a centralized configuration data store for named values called parameters.`,`Parameter Store supports the following parameter types: String Use this type for plain text values, such as environment names, endpoint URLs, or resource identifiers.`,`StringList Use this type for a comma-separated list of plain-text values.`,`SecureString Use SecureString for configuration values that require encryption, such as service endpoints and account identifiers.`,`For secrets such as database credentials, API keys, or tokens, we recommend AWS Secrets Manager, which provides purpose built security controls including automatic rotation and cross-region replication.`])],services:[`AWS Secrets Manager`,`AWS Systems Manager`,`AWS KMS`],design:[n(`Keep credentials out of application source code: store them in Secrets Manager and rotate them. Secrets Manager uses IAM to secure access to secrets, so the application's role can be given permission to retrieve the secrets it needs.`,[`secrets-intro`,`secrets-rotation`,`secrets-access`],[`secrets-intro|Storing the credentials in Secrets Manager helps avoid possible compromise by anyone who can inspect your application or the components.`,`secrets-rotation|In Secrets Manager, you can set up automatic rotation for your secrets.`,`secrets-access|Secrets Manager uses AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) to secure access to secrets.`])]},{id:`1.2-K4`,concepts:[n(`Amazon Cognito answers two different questions. A user pool answers "who is this user?" (authentication): it is a user directory for web and mobile app sign-up and sign-in, and from your app's perspective an OpenID Connect identity provider. An identity pool answers "what AWS resources may they use?" (authorization): it exchanges identities for temporary AWS credentials.`,[`cognito-what-is`,`cognito-user-pools`,`cognito-identity-pools`],[`cognito-what-is|Amazon Cognito answers two different questions.`,`cognito-what-is|(authentication) Use a user pool.`,`cognito-what-is|(authorization) Use an identity pool.`,`cognito-user-pools|An Amazon Cognito user pool is a user directory for web and mobile app authentication and authorization.`,`cognito-user-pools|From the perspective of your app, an Amazon Cognito user pool is an OpenID Connect (OIDC) identity provider (IdP).`,`cognito-identity-pools|Identity pools generate temporary AWS credentials for the users of your app, whether they’ve signed in or you haven’t identified them yet.`],{allow:[`two`]}),n(`User pools can also sign users in through external identity providers, and Cognito can authenticate users from its built-in directory, an enterprise directory or consumer providers such as Google and Facebook.`,[`cognito-what-is`,`cognito-user-pools`],[`cognito-what-is|from the built-in user directory, from your enterprise directory, and from consumer identity providers like Google and Facebook.`,`cognito-user-pools|including sign-in through external identity providers`]),n(`AWS IAM Identity Center is the AWS solution for connecting workforce users to AWS managed applications and other AWS resources, whereas a Cognito user pool is a user directory for the sign-up and sign-in of an application's own users.`,[`sso-what-is`,`cognito-user-pools`],[`sso-what-is|AWS IAM Identity Center is the AWS solution for connecting your workforce users to AWS managed applications`,`cognito-user-pools|An Amazon Cognito user pool is a user directory for web and mobile app authentication and authorization.`]),n(`AWS WAF can also protect an Amazon Cognito user pool: it is one of the resource types in the AWS WAF developer guide.`,`waf-what-is`,[`Amazon Cognito user pool`])],services:[`Amazon Cognito`,`AWS IAM Identity Center`],design:[n(`End users of a mobile or web app who need to sign up, sign in, or use social login: Cognito user pool. Those users then need to call an AWS service such as S3 or DynamoDB directly: add a Cognito identity pool for temporary credentials. Employees accessing AWS accounts and workforce applications: IAM Identity Center.`,[`cognito-what-is`,`cognito-identity-pools`,`sso-what-is`],[`cognito-what-is|(authentication) Use a user pool.`,`cognito-identity-pools|Sign API requests to AWS services like Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) and Amazon DynamoDB.`,`sso-what-is|AWS IAM Identity Center is the AWS solution for connecting your workforce users to AWS managed applications`])]}],cues:[e(`credentials must not be hard-coded / rotate database passwords automatically`,`AWS Secrets Manager`,n(`Secrets Manager removes the need for hard-coded credentials and can rotate secrets on a schedule.`,`secrets-intro`,[`Secrets Manager helps you improve your security posture, because you no longer need hard-coded credentials in application source code.`,`With Secrets Manager, you can configure an automatic rotation schedule for your secrets.`])),e(`store configuration values (endpoints, flags), optionally encrypted`,`Systems Manager Parameter Store`,n(`Parameter Store is a centralized configuration data store; SecureString covers values that need encryption.`,`ssm-parameter-store`,[`SecureString Use SecureString for configuration values that require encryption, such as service endpoints and account identifiers.`])),e(`sign-up and sign-in for a mobile or web app / social identity providers`,`Amazon Cognito user pool`,n(`A user pool is a user directory for web and mobile app authentication and authorization.`,`cognito-user-pools`,[`An Amazon Cognito user pool is a user directory for web and mobile app authentication and authorization.`])),e(`give app users temporary AWS credentials to call S3 or DynamoDB directly`,`Amazon Cognito identity pool`,n(`Identity pools generate temporary AWS credentials for the users of your app.`,`cognito-identity-pools`,[`Identity pools generate temporary AWS credentials for the users of your app`])),e(`workforce / employees single sign-on to AWS accounts and applications`,`AWS IAM Identity Center`,n(`IAM Identity Center connects workforce users to AWS applications and resources.`,`sso-what-is`,[`AWS IAM Identity Center is the AWS solution for connecting your workforce users to AWS managed applications`]))],examples:[],confuse:[`secrets-manager-vs-parameter-store`],azure:[{concept:`Microsoft Entra External ID`,aws:`Amazon Cognito`,mapping:n(`For customer-facing apps, Learn pairs Cognito with the Microsoft identity platform and Entra External ID, which serves the same purpose: signing in the customers of your application.`,[`learn-hub`,`cognito-user-pools`],[`learn-hub|External ID is a highly available global identity management service for consumer-facing`,`cognito-user-pools|An Amazon Cognito user pool is a user directory for web and mobile app authentication and authorization.`]),breaks:n(`Cognito is two things. A user pool authenticates users, and an identity pool exchanges identities for temporary AWS credentials; the Learn comparison for External ID describes a global identity management service for consumer-facing applications and does not mention temporary AWS credentials.`,[`cognito-what-is`,`cognito-identity-pools`,`learn-hub`],[`cognito-what-is|Amazon Cognito answers two different questions.`,`cognito-identity-pools|Identity pools generate temporary AWS credentials for the users of your app`,`learn-hub|Microsoft identity platform and Microsoft Entra External ID`,`learn-hub|External ID is a highly available global identity management service for consumer-facing`],{allow:[`two`]})},{concept:`Microsoft Entra ID`,aws:`AWS IAM Identity Center`,mapping:n(`For employees, Learn pairs Entra ID with IAM Identity Center and IAM, the services for controlling access to services and resources.`,[`learn-hub`,`sso-what-is`],[`learn-hub|Use these services to more securely control access to services and resources and improve data security and protection.`,`sso-what-is|AWS IAM Identity Center is the AWS solution for connecting your workforce users to AWS managed applications`]),breaks:n(`AWS describes IAM Identity Center as the solution for connecting workforce users to AWS managed applications and other AWS resources, whereas it describes Cognito as authenticating and authorizing users from a built-in user directory, an enterprise directory or consumer identity providers. Learn lists only Microsoft Entra ID against IAM Identity Center.`,[`sso-what-is`,`cognito-what-is`,`learn-hub`],[`learn-hub|Microsoft Entra ID`,`sso-what-is|AWS IAM Identity Center is the AWS solution for connecting your workforce users to AWS managed applications`,`cognito-what-is|With Amazon Cognito, you can authenticate and authorize users from the built-in user directory, from your enterprise directory, and from consumer identity providers like Google and Facebook.`])}]},m={building:`watchtower`,overview:[n(`Threats to a workload include flooding a site with traffic (a {{term:ddos}} attack), injecting malicious SQL into a web request, leaked credentials, or sensitive data sitting in a public bucket. This building is the set of AWS services that block or detect those things: AWS Shield and AWS WAF, Amazon GuardDuty, Amazon Macie and Amazon Inspector for detection and assessment, AWS Security Hub CSPM to receive findings from other services, and Amazon Detective to investigate them.`,[`shield-overview`,`waf-sqli`,`waf-what-is`,`guardduty`,`macie`,`inspector`,`security-hub`,`detective`],[`shield-overview|A DDoS attack is an attack in which multiple compromised systems try to flood a target with traffic.`,`waf-sqli|Attackers insert malicious SQL code into web requests in order to do things like modify your database or extract data from it.`,`guardduty|Amazon GuardDuty is a threat detection service that continuously monitors, analyzes, and processes AWS data sources and logs in your AWS environment.`,`macie|Amazon Macie is a data security service that discovers sensitive data by using machine learning and pattern matching`,`inspector|Amazon Inspector is a vulnerability management service that automatically discovers workloads and continually scans them for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure.`,`security-hub|Security Hub CSPM also receives findings from other AWS services—such as Amazon GuardDuty, Amazon Inspector, and Amazon Macie`,`detective|Investigate and respond to security findings with streamlined visualizations.`],{newer:[`security-hub-unified`]})],beyondProject:[n(`If your static site is delivered through CloudFront, CloudFront is one of the resource types AWS WAF can protect; the AWS WAF guide also lists Amazon API Gateway REST APIs, so an API Gateway HTTP API is not on that list. SAA adds the design choices: put a web ACL on the distribution or REST API to filter requests, and know that Shield Standard gives every AWS customer automatic DDoS protection at no additional charge.`,[`waf-what-is`,`shield-standard`],[`waf-what-is|You can protect the following resource types: Amazon CloudFront distributionAmazon API Gateway REST APIApplication Load Balancer`,`shield-standard|All AWS customers benefit from the automatic protection of Shield Standard, at no additional charge.`])],bullets:[{id:`1.2-K5`,concepts:[n(`{{term:guardduty|Amazon GuardDuty}} is a threat detection service that continuously monitors AWS data sources and logs, using threat intelligence feeds and machine learning to identify suspicious activity such as compromised AWS credentials. Its foundational data sources are CloudTrail management events, VPC Flow Logs and Route 53 Resolver DNS query logs.`,[`guardduty`,`guardduty-data`],[`guardduty|GuardDuty uses threat intelligence feeds, such as lists of malicious IP addresses and domains, file hashes, and machine learning (ML) models to identify suspicious and potentially malicious activity in your AWS environment.`,`guardduty|Compromised and exfiltrated AWS credentials.`,`guardduty-data|AWS CloudTrail management eventsVPC Flow LogsRoute53 Resolver DNS query logs`]),n(`{{term:macie}} discovers sensitive data in Amazon S3 using machine learning and pattern matching, and generates a finding when it detects sensitive data or a security issue such as a bucket that becomes publicly accessible.`,`macie`,[`Amazon Macie is a data security service that discovers sensitive data by using machine learning and pattern matching`,`Macie generates a finding for you to review and remediate as necessary.`,`such as a bucket that becomes publicly accessible`]),n(`Amazon Inspector is a vulnerability management service that continually scans EC2 instances, container images in Amazon ECR and Lambda functions for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure.`,`inspector`,[`Amazon Inspector is a vulnerability management service that automatically discovers workloads and continually scans them for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure.`,`Amazon Inspector discovers and scans Amazon EC2 instances, container images in Amazon ECR, and Lambda functions.`]),n(`AWS Security Hub CSPM (Cloud Security Posture Management) gives a comprehensive view of your security state: it runs checks against security controls and also receives findings from services such as GuardDuty, Inspector and Macie. Amazon Detective collects log data and uses machine learning, statistical analysis and graph theory to help you investigate findings.`,[`security-hub`,`detective`],[`security-hub|AWS Security Hub Cloud Security Posture Management (AWS Security Hub CSPM) provides you with a comprehensive view of your security state in AWS`,`security-hub|In addition to generating control findings, Security Hub CSPM also receives findings from other AWS services—such as Amazon GuardDuty, Amazon Inspector, and Amazon Macie`,`detective|Amazon Detective automatically collects log data from your AWS resources and uses machine learning (ML), statistical analysis, and graph theory to build a dataset that you can use to conduct more efficient security investigations.`],{newer:[`security-hub-unified`]}),n(`Amazon Cognito handles user sign-in and access for applications; user pools and identity pools are covered in Warden's Lodge.`,`cognito-what-is`,[`With Amazon Cognito, you can authenticate and authorize users from the built-in user directory, from your enterprise directory, and from consumer identity providers like Google and Facebook.`]),n(`AWS now describes separate Security Hub products. AWS Security Hub CSPM provides automated security best practice checks to help you understand your security posture across accounts. The newer AWS Security Hub aggregates and correlates signals from Amazon GuardDuty, Amazon Inspector, AWS Security Hub CSPM and Amazon Macie, organising them by threats, exposures, resources and security coverage.`,[`securityhub-faq`,`securityhub-ga`],[`securityhub-faq|Security Hub CSPM (Cloud Security Posture Management) provides automated security best practice checks to help you understand your overall security posture across your AWS accounts.`,`securityhub-ga|Available for individual accounts or entire AWS Organizations accounts, Security Hub automatically aggregates and correlates signals from Amazon GuardDuty, Amazon Inspector, AWS Security Hub Cloud Security Posture Management (AWS Security Hub CSPM), and Amazon Macie, organizing them by threats, exposures, resources, and security coverage.`],{newer:[`security-hub-unified`]})],services:[`Amazon GuardDuty`,`Amazon Macie`,`Amazon Inspector`,`AWS Security Hub`,`Amazon Detective`,`Amazon Cognito`],design:[n(`Match the service to the question. Suspicious activity or compromised credentials in the account: GuardDuty. Sensitive data in S3 or public buckets: Macie. Software vulnerabilities and network exposure on workloads: Inspector. One place that aggregates findings and security-check results: Security Hub CSPM. Digging into the cause of a finding: Detective.`,[`guardduty`,`macie`,`inspector`,`security-hub`,`detective`],[`guardduty|Amazon GuardDuty is a threat detection service that continuously monitors, analyzes, and processes AWS data sources and logs in your AWS environment.`,`macie|Amazon Macie is a data security service that discovers sensitive data by using machine learning and pattern matching`,`inspector|continually scans them for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure`,`security-hub|Security Hub CSPM runs checks against security controls and generates control findings to help you assess your compliance against security best practices.`,`detective|Determine potential security issues through a unified view of user and resource interactions.`],{newer:[`security-hub-unified`]})]},{id:`1.2-K6`,concepts:[n(`A {{term:ddos}} attack is one in which multiple compromised systems try to flood a target with traffic. Shield Standard defends against the most common network and transport layer DDoS attacks. For floods of web requests at the application layer, AWS WAF has rate-based rules, which count incoming requests and rate limit them when they come at too fast a rate, and Shield Advanced can be configured to respond automatically with application layer DDoS mitigation, which adds a rule group to your protection pack (web ACL).`,[`shield-overview`,`shield-standard`,`waf-rate-based`,`shield-capabilities`],[`shield-overview|A DDoS attack is an attack in which multiple compromised systems try to flood a target with traffic.`,`shield-standard|Shield Standard defends against the most common, frequently occurring network and transport layer DDoS attacks that target your website or applications.`,`waf-rate-based|A rate-based rule counts incoming requests and rate limits requests when they are coming at too fast a rate.`,`shield-capabilities|Enabling Shield Advanced automatic application layer DDoS mitigation adds a rule group to your protection pack (web ACL)`,`shield-capabilities|You can configure Shield Advanced to respond automatically to mitigate application layer (layer 7) attacks against your protected resources.`]),n(`SQL injection is an attack where malicious SQL code is inserted into web requests to modify or extract data from a database. AWS WAF has a rule statement that inspects requests for malicious SQL code.`,`waf-sqli`,[`Attackers insert malicious SQL code into web requests in order to do things like modify your database or extract data from it.`,`An SQL injection rule statement inspects for malicious SQL code.`]),n(`Shield Advanced comes with DDoS cost protection: it safeguards against scaling charges resulting from DDoS-related usage spikes on protected EC2, ELB, CloudFront, Global Accelerator and Route 53 resources, and if a protected resource scales up in response to an attack you can request Shield Advanced service credits through the regular AWS Support channel.`,`shield-features`,[`DDoS cost protection AWS Shield Advanced comes with DDoS cost protection to safeguard against scaling charges resulting from DDoS-related usage spikes on protected EC2, ELB, CloudFront, Global Accelerator, and Route 53 resources.`,`If any of these protected resources scale up in response to a DDoS attack, you can request Shield Advanced service credits through your regular AWS Support channel.`])],services:[`AWS Shield`,`AWS WAF`],design:[n(`AWS Shield addresses DDoS, and AWS WAF filters web requests for a protected resource such as a CloudFront distribution, an Application Load Balancer or an API Gateway REST API.`,[`shield-standard`,`waf-what-is`],[`shield-standard|Shield Standard defends against the most common, frequently occurring network and transport layer DDoS attacks that target your website or applications.`,`waf-what-is|You can protect the following resource types: Amazon CloudFront distributionAmazon API Gateway REST APIApplication Load Balancer`])]},{id:`1.2-S3`,concepts:[n("AWS WAF works by defining a web access control list (web ACL) and associating it with the web application resources you want to protect. Requests are forwarded to AWS WAF for inspection by the web ACL, and the protected resource responds with the content, a `403`, or a custom response.",[`waf-how-works`,`waf-what-is`],[`waf-how-works|You do this by defining a web access control list (web ACL) and then associating it with one or more web application resources that you want to protect.`,`waf-how-works|The associated resources forward incoming requests to AWS WAF for inspection by the web ACL.`,`waf-what-is|with an HTTP 403 status code (Forbidden), or with a custom response`]),n(`AWS Shield Standard gives all AWS customers automatic protection at no additional charge against common network and transport layer DDoS attacks. Shield Advanced is a subscription for expanded protection of the resources you choose: it does not protect resources automatically, it can be used with resource types such as CloudFront distributions, Route 53 hosted zones, Global Accelerator standard accelerators, Elastic IP addresses and Application Load Balancers, its subscription covers the costs of using standard AWS WAF capabilities for the resources you protect, and, with a Business Support or Enterprise Support plan, it gives access to the AWS Shield Response Team.`,[`shield-standard`,`shield-advanced`,`shield-protected-resources`,`shield-capabilities`],[`shield-standard|All AWS customers benefit from the automatic protection of Shield Standard, at no additional charge.`,`shield-standard|Shield Standard defends against the most common, frequently occurring network and transport layer DDoS attacks that target your website or applications.`,`shield-advanced|For higher levels of protection against attacks, you can subscribe to AWS Shield Advanced.`,`shield-protected-resources|Shield Advanced doesn't automatically protect your resources.`,`shield-protected-resources|You can use Shield Advanced for advanced monitoring and protection with the following resource types:`,`shield-protected-resources|CloudFront distributions.`,`shield-protected-resources|Amazon Route 53 hosted zones.`,`shield-protected-resources|AWS Global Accelerator standard accelerators.`,`shield-protected-resources|Amazon EC2 Elastic IP addresses.`,`shield-protected-resources|Application Load Balancers.`,`shield-capabilities|Your Shield Advanced subscription covers the costs of using standard AWS WAF capabilities for resources that you protect with Shield Advanced.`,`shield-capabilities|AWS Shield Response Team (SRT)`,`shield-capabilities|must also be subscribed to the Business Support plan or the Enterprise Support plan.`]),n(`AWS Network Firewall is a stateful, managed network firewall and intrusion detection and prevention service for your VPC. Among its uses, you can pass traffic through only from known AWS service domains or IP address endpoints, and use custom lists of known bad domains to limit the domain names that your applications can access.`,`network-firewall`,[`AWS Network Firewall is a stateful, managed, network firewall and intrusion detection and prevention service for your virtual private cloud (VPC)`,`Pass traffic through only from known AWS service domains or IP address endpoints, such as Amazon S3.`,`Use custom lists of known bad domains to limit the types of domain names that your applications can access.`]),n(`AWS Network Firewall filters traffic at the perimeter of your VPC and uses the open source intrusion prevention system Suricata for stateful inspection. AWS Firewall Manager applies protections such as AWS WAF, Shield Advanced, security groups and Network Firewall across multiple accounts from one place.`,[`network-firewall`,`firewall-manager`],[`network-firewall|With Network Firewall, you can filter traffic at the perimeter of your VPC.`,`network-firewall|Network Firewall uses the open source intrusion prevention system (IPS), Suricata, for stateful inspection`,`firewall-manager|AWS Firewall Manager simplifies your administration and maintenance tasks across multiple accounts and resources for a variety of protections, including AWS WAF, AWS Shield Advanced, Amazon VPC security groups and network ACLs, AWS Network Firewall`]),n(`Application credentials are handled by other services: AWS Secrets Manager stores and rotates them (see Warden's Lodge), and IAM Identity Center connects workforce users to AWS.`,[`secrets-intro`,`sso-what-is`],[`AWS Secrets Manager helps you manage, retrieve, and rotate database credentials, application credentials, OAuth tokens, API keys, and other secrets throughout their lifecycles.`,`sso-what-is|AWS IAM Identity Center is the AWS solution for connecting your workforce users to AWS managed applications`])],services:[`AWS Shield`,`AWS WAF`,`AWS Network Firewall`,`AWS Firewall Manager`,`AWS IAM Identity Center`,`AWS Secrets Manager`],design:[n(`Layer the defences: Shield for DDoS, a WAF web ACL on a resource such as a CloudFront distribution, Application Load Balancer or API Gateway REST API for request filtering, security groups and network ACLs inside the VPC, and Network Firewall when you need to inspect traffic at the VPC perimeter. Use Firewall Manager to apply these consistently across accounts.`,[`shield-standard`,`waf-what-is`,`vpc-security-groups`,`vpc-nacls`,`waf-how-works`,`network-firewall`,`firewall-manager`],[`shield-standard|Shield Standard defends against the most common, frequently occurring network and transport layer DDoS attacks that target your website or applications.`,`waf-what-is|You can protect the following resource types: Amazon CloudFront distributionAmazon API Gateway REST APIApplication Load Balancer`,`vpc-security-groups|A security group controls the traffic that is allowed to reach and leave the resources that it is associated with.`,`vpc-nacls|A network access control list (ACL) allows or denies specific inbound or outbound traffic at the subnet level.`,`waf-how-works|You use AWS WAF to control how your protected resources respond to HTTP(S) web requests.`,`network-firewall|With Network Firewall, you can filter traffic at the perimeter of your VPC.`,`firewall-manager|With Firewall Manager, you set up your protections just once and the service automatically applies them across your accounts and resources`])]}],cues:[e(`DDoS attack / network or transport layer flood`,`AWS Shield (Standard is automatic; Advanced for expanded protection)`,n(`Shield Standard automatically protects all AWS customers against common network and transport layer DDoS attacks; Advanced is a subscription for higher levels of protection.`,[`shield-standard`,`shield-advanced`],[`shield-standard|Shield Standard defends against the most common, frequently occurring network and transport layer DDoS attacks that target your website or applications.`,`shield-advanced|For higher levels of protection against attacks, you can subscribe to AWS Shield Advanced.`])),e(`flood of HTTP requests / too many requests from one source`,`an AWS WAF rate-based rule (or Shield Advanced automatic application layer mitigation)`,n(`An AWS WAF rate-based rule counts incoming requests and rate limits them when they come at too fast a rate; Shield Advanced can be configured to respond automatically with application layer DDoS mitigation.`,[`waf-rate-based`,`shield-capabilities`],[`waf-rate-based|A rate-based rule counts incoming requests and rate limits requests when they are coming at too fast a rate.`,`shield-capabilities|Enabling Shield Advanced automatic application layer DDoS mitigation adds a rule group to your protection pack (web ACL)`,`shield-capabilities|You can configure Shield Advanced to respond automatically to mitigate application layer (layer 7) attacks against your protected resources.`])),e(`filter traffic by domain name or inspect it with intrusion prevention rules in the VPC`,`AWS Network Firewall`,n(`Network Firewall uses the open source intrusion prevention system Suricata for stateful inspection, and you can use custom lists of known bad domains to limit the domain names your applications can access.`,`network-firewall`,[`Network Firewall uses the open source intrusion prevention system (IPS), Suricata, for stateful inspection, and supports Suricata compatible rules.`,`Use custom lists of known bad domains to limit the types of domain names that your applications can access.`])),e(`SQL injection / filter HTTP requests / block IP addresses at the application layer`,`AWS WAF`,n(`AWS WAF lets you monitor and control HTTP(S) requests to protected resources, for example by IP address or query-string values.`,`waf-what-is`,[`Based on criteria that you specify, such as the IP addresses that requests originate from or the values of query strings`])),e(`detect compromised credentials / suspicious activity / communication with malicious IP addresses`,`Amazon GuardDuty`,n(`GuardDuty identifies suspicious activity using threat intelligence feeds such as lists of malicious IP addresses and domains, and machine learning, and covers compromised credentials.`,`guardduty`,[`GuardDuty uses threat intelligence feeds, such as lists of malicious IP addresses and domains, file hashes, and machine learning (ML) models to identify suspicious and potentially malicious activity in your AWS environment.`,`Compromised and exfiltrated AWS credentials.`])),e(`discover sensitive data (PII) in Amazon S3`,`Amazon Macie`,n(`Macie discovers sensitive data in S3 using machine learning and pattern matching.`,`macie`,[`Amazon Macie is a data security service that discovers sensitive data by using machine learning and pattern matching`])),e(`scan EC2 instances, container images or Lambda functions for software vulnerabilities`,`Amazon Inspector`,n(`Amazon Inspector discovers and scans EC2 instances, container images in Amazon ECR and Lambda functions.`,`inspector`,[`Amazon Inspector discovers and scans Amazon EC2 instances, container images in Amazon ECR, and Lambda functions.`])),e(`aggregate security findings from several services in one place`,`AWS Security Hub CSPM`,n(`Security Hub also receives findings from other AWS services such as GuardDuty, Inspector and Macie.`,`security-hub`,[`Security Hub CSPM also receives findings from other AWS services—such as Amazon GuardDuty, Amazon Inspector, and Amazon Macie`])),e(`apply WAF or Shield Advanced protections across all accounts`,`AWS Firewall Manager`,n(`Firewall Manager applies protections across accounts and resources.`,`firewall-manager`,[`With Firewall Manager, you set up your protections just once and the service automatically applies them across your accounts and resources`]))],examples:[],confuse:[`shield-vs-waf`],azure:[{concept:`Azure web application firewall`,aws:`AWS WAF`,mapping:n(`Both are web application firewalls for protecting web apps from common exploits.`,[`learn-hub`,`waf-what-is`],[`learn-hub|These firewalls help protect web applications from common web exploits.`,`waf-what-is|AWS WAF is a web application firewall that lets you monitor the HTTP(S) requests that are forwarded to your protected web application resources.`]),breaks:n(`Learn describes the Azure firewalls as helping protect web applications from common web exploits and does not say where they are deployed. In AWS, you define a web ACL and associate it with the web application resources to protect, such as a CloudFront distribution, an API Gateway REST API or an Application Load Balancer.`,[`waf-what-is`,`waf-how-works`,`learn-hub`],[`waf-what-is|You can protect the following resource types: Amazon CloudFront distributionAmazon API Gateway REST APIApplication Load Balancer`,`learn-hub|AWS WAF Azure web application firewall`,`learn-hub|These firewalls help protect web applications from common web exploits.`,`waf-how-works|by defining a web access control list (web ACL) and then associating it with one or more web application resources that you want to protect`])},{concept:`Azure DDoS Protection`,aws:`AWS Shield`,mapping:n(`Both protect cloud services from distributed denial of service attacks: Learn says the Azure services help protect cloud services from them, and AWS says Shield Standard and Shield Advanced provide protections against DDoS attacks for AWS resources.`,[`learn-hub`,`shield-overview`],[`learn-hub|These services help you protect your cloud services from distributed denial of service attacks.`,`shield-overview|AWS Shield Standard and AWS Shield Advanced provide protections against Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) attacks for AWS resources at the network and transport layers (layer 3 and 4) and the application layer (layer 7).`]),breaks:n(`AWS has two named tiers. Shield Standard protects every AWS customer automatically at no additional charge, and Shield Advanced is a subscription for expanded protection. The Learn comparison describes Azure DDoS Protection only as helping protect cloud services from distributed denial of service attacks; it does not describe tiers.`,[`shield-standard`,`shield-advanced`,`learn-hub`],[`shield-standard|All AWS customers benefit from the automatic protection of Shield Standard, at no additional charge.`,`shield-advanced|For higher levels of protection against attacks, you can subscribe to AWS Shield Advanced.`,`learn-hub|These services help you protect your cloud services from distributed denial of service attacks.`],{allow:[`two`]})},{concept:`Microsoft Sentinel`,aws:`Amazon GuardDuty`,mapping:n(`The Learn comparison pairs GuardDuty with Microsoft Sentinel because both are about detecting attacks.`,[`learn-hub`,`guardduty`],[`learn-hub|Detect and investigate advanced attacks on-premises and in the cloud.`,`guardduty|Amazon GuardDuty is a threat detection service that continuously monitors, analyzes, and processes AWS data sources and logs in your AWS environment.`]),breaks:n(`Learn describes Microsoft Sentinel as detecting and investigating advanced attacks on-premises and in the cloud. In AWS the work is split: GuardDuty is a threat detection service that analyses data sources such as CloudTrail management events, VPC Flow Logs and DNS query logs, AWS Security Hub CSPM receives findings from other AWS services, and Amazon Detective supports investigating findings.`,[`guardduty-data`,`security-hub`,`detective`,`learn-hub`],[`guardduty-data|AWS CloudTrail management eventsVPC Flow LogsRoute53 Resolver DNS query logs`,`security-hub|Security Hub CSPM also receives findings from other AWS services—such as Amazon GuardDuty, Amazon Inspector, and Amazon Macie`,`detective|Investigate and respond to security findings with streamlined visualizations.`,`learn-hub|Detect and investigate advanced attacks on-premises and in the cloud.`],{newer:[`security-hub-unified`]})},{concept:`Microsoft Defender for Cloud`,aws:`Amazon Inspector`,mapping:n(`Learn pairs Inspector with Defender for Cloud: both assess workloads for security weaknesses.`,[`learn-hub`,`inspector`],[`learn-hub|Defender for Cloud is an automated security assessment service that improves the security and compliance of applications`,`inspector|Amazon Inspector is a vulnerability management service that automatically discovers workloads and continually scans them for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure.`]),breaks:n(`Learn describes Defender for Cloud as assessing applications for vulnerabilities or deviations from best practices. In AWS those assessments sit in two services: Inspector scans specific resource types (EC2 instances, container images in ECR and Lambda functions) for vulnerabilities, and AWS Security Hub CSPM runs checks against security controls.`,[`inspector`,`security-hub`,`learn-hub`],[`inspector|Amazon Inspector discovers and scans Amazon EC2 instances, container images in Amazon ECR, and Lambda functions.`,`security-hub|Security Hub CSPM runs checks against security controls and generates control findings to help you assess your compliance against security best practices.`,`learn-hub|It automatically assesses applications for vulnerabilities or deviations from best practices.`],{newer:[`security-hub-unified`],allow:[`two`]})},{concept:`Azure Firewall`,aws:`AWS Network Firewall`,mapping:n(`Microsoft Learn describes Azure Firewall as a managed, stateful, cloud-native firewall similar to AWS Network Firewall.`,[`learn-networking`,`network-firewall`],[`learn-networking|Azure Firewall provides a managed, stateful, cloud-native firewall with FQDN filtering, threat intelligence, and optional IDPS/TLS inspection, similar to AWS Network Firewall.`,`network-firewall|With Network Firewall, you can filter traffic at the perimeter of your VPC.`]),breaks:n(`Learn lists FQDN filtering, threat intelligence and optional IDPS and TLS inspection for Azure Firewall. AWS Network Firewall is described as filtering traffic at the perimeter of a VPC, and as using the open source intrusion prevention system Suricata for stateful inspection with Suricata compatible rules.`,[`network-firewall`,`learn-networking`],[`learn-networking|Azure Firewall provides a managed, stateful, cloud-native firewall with FQDN filtering, threat intelligence, and optional IDPS/TLS inspection, similar to AWS Network Firewall.`,`network-firewall|With Network Firewall, you can filter traffic at the perimeter of your VPC.`,`network-firewall|Network Firewall uses the open source intrusion prevention system (IPS), Suricata, for stateful inspection, and supports Suricata compatible rules.`])}]},h=Object.values(Object.assign({"../../content/notes/citadel/archive-annex.ts":r,"../../content/notes/citadel/certificate-office.ts":i,"../../content/notes/citadel/charter-hall.ts":a,"../../content/notes/citadel/compliance-registry.ts":o,"../../content/notes/citadel/customs-house.ts":s,"../../content/notes/citadel/embassy-row.ts":c,"../../content/notes/citadel/federation-bridge.ts":l,"../../content/notes/citadel/gatehouse.ts":u,"../../content/notes/citadel/identity-keep.ts":d,"../../content/notes/citadel/key-vault.ts":f,"../../content/notes/citadel/wardens-lodge.ts":p,"../../content/notes/citadel/watchtower.ts":m}));export{h as notes};